WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.600
JEFF KEMBER: Hello, hey,
thanks for coming today, guys.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.490
Appreciate everyone being here
this early in the morning.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.740
If I could get everybody just
to kind of move on up, please.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.000
So we can get the group
a little bit tighter.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.390
That'd be great, please.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.130
And sorry we're a
couple minutes late

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:16.338
getting rolling this morning.

00:00:16.338 --> 00:00:19.640
But again, thank you
all for being here.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.980
My name's Jeff Kember.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:26.110
I am a Cloud Solutions
Architect with the GCP team.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:28.370
And in a former life, I
was a CG sup at Framestore.

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:29.870
And I've had the
pleasure of working

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.779
at companies such as Blizzard
and Island and Pixar.

00:00:32.779 --> 00:00:35.070
We're going to have a couple
different speakers come up

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.540
and chat with you today.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.610
I wanted to introduce a
new Solutions Architect

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:40.570
that we have.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.840
So I have a media background
from the film industry.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.650
And our latest hire, his
second week with the company,

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.560
Adrian Graham in the
red shirt over there.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:49.935
Please give Adrian
a big welcome.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.560
For those of you
who know Adrian,

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.560
he is former Digital Domain
in this very building.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.040
We worked together here, and
he spent the last couple years

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.320
at Autodesk working on Bifrost
and other cool next-generation

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.040
Maya technology.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.750
So this really is, I think,
an additional statement

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:09.550
on Google's emphasis
to this market

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.760
vertical that they're
employing people like us

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:13.760
to do this kind of work.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.343
So really excited
to work with Adrian,

00:01:15.343 --> 00:01:18.150
and he's based here
in Venice office.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:20.940
So Google's Cloud
offering, we have

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.480
a variety of custom solutions,
and we also have Zync.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.396
And I'm sure most people
are familiar with it,

00:01:25.396 --> 00:01:28.460
but I will go through it
and talk just a little bit

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:29.200
about it.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.230
It's a fantastic Cloud
rendering solution

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.060
for those who have
the opportunity

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.940
to be able to work with a clean
sheet version of Maya or Nuke.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.720
We have a Cloud rendering
solution for you.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.620
It allows you to
render on demand,

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.900
and it enables-- there's
a Cloud file system.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.220
Your files get uploaded.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.270
And licensing is
handled for you.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:54.380
So any of these products are
available on a per minute

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.240
demand basis.

00:01:58.489 --> 00:02:00.030
The way the architecture
works is you

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.270
have an open source
plug-in that you download,

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.520
and it works with Maya
and Nuke currently.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:07.020
And you're able to
take your scene,

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.139
encapsulate it, and put
it up onto the Cloud.

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.690
Your files are stored in
a persistent file system.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:14.300
And the render notes
get spun up for you,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.250
and the pictures come back.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:22.040
The architecture is such that
once the files are uploaded,

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.530
only the change occurs.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:27.190
With Maya 2016, the
bin files get uploaded

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:28.500
from your local scene.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.700
And all of the render
creation occurs on the Cloud.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.060
So you don't have to do
local writing of ASS files

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.040
and such for Arnold,
for instance.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.660
And the same scenario
works with Nuke.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.350
So renders that already
exists in the cloud, when

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.380
you want to run a new comp for
all elements that have already

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:47.838
been rendered in
the Cloud, they're

00:02:47.838 --> 00:02:50.060
already persistent there.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.050
Your final comp
comes back to you.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.740
So it's a really great
solution for smaller facilities

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.820
that don't have custom plug-ins
and their own pipeline.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:59.240
This gives artists
the opportunity

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:01.770
to scale significantly.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.080
This is what the
interface looks like.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:05.890
I've already described
how it works.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:09.910
This is the, basically,
queuing control engine

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:12.120
that lets you see what
the progress of your job

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.190
is and see how many render
nodes you're running,

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.320
and how they're
progressing in your job.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.510
So here are a number of films
that were created using Zync.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:24.005
And we're quite happy
with the amount of success

00:03:24.005 --> 00:03:25.130
in the marketplace with it.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:29.718
So we encourage you to try it
if you fit the use case for it.

00:03:29.718 --> 00:03:34.100
For medium and large
studios, we have a framework

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.894
for creating custom solutions.

00:03:35.894 --> 00:03:37.310
And these are the
quick topics I'm

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:38.530
going to run through the day.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:39.029
It's a lot.

00:03:39.029 --> 00:03:41.200
I'll run through it
as quickly as I can.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.680
From an ingest standpoint,
we're peered significantly

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:48.100
with most major
markets on the planet.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.300
We're here in One Wiltshire
and multiple locations

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.290
here in Los Angeles.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.830
So your ability to get data into
the Cloud and get it back out

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:59.090
again quickly with
Google's global network,

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.190
we've leased ships and
laid fibre under the ocean.

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:04.540
So if you have other facilities
in London or Singapore

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.290
or Australia or Canada,
we have the ability

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.580
to be able to jump on
and off our network,

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:12.560
but it's private the
entire time it's there.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.001
So normally, connectivity
is going to be over a VPN.

00:04:15.001 --> 00:04:17.459
And you're going to either do
that over the public internet

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:20.019
or with a CDI, a Carrier
Direct Interconnect,

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:21.880
or letting up dark fibre.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:24.090
Either CDI or direct
connect, you get

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.780
a 66% reduction in egress fees.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.090
So that is significant.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:31.506
If you're in a position where
you can light up dark fibre,

00:04:31.506 --> 00:04:33.010
we certainly encourage
you to do so.

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:34.565
We have a Google
managed VPN solution

00:04:34.565 --> 00:04:36.690
where we manage the threads
and throughput for you.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.170
So it's just plug and
play, and it works.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.920
Initially, we had a conflict
where it didn't work

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.355
with Avere, and now it does.

00:04:43.355 --> 00:04:45.480
That's been rolled out, so
you can use our product,

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:46.770
or you can roll your own.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:51.040
Any open source
IPsec VPN will work.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.800
So the fabric that we run
on is really kind of cool.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:57.100
Google buys roughly 20--
it's like more than 20%

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:58.920
of Intel's global production.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.980
The next closest
Cloud provider buys 1%

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.750
of Intel's global production.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:07.020
A significant portion
of the Intel processors

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:10.610
that we buy go into our
software defined networking.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:13.350
If you want to know a
little more about it,

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.610
released last summer, there's
a paper on Jupiter fabric.

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:17.960
And it's circa 2012.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.970
So it's a snapshot of what the
technology was at that time.

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:23.552
We're obviously a generation
or two newer than that now,

00:05:23.552 --> 00:05:24.760
but it's a really cool paper.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:27.241
And it offers photographs
of our machine racks

00:05:27.241 --> 00:05:29.490
in the data center that you
don't normally get to see.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.500
And it also offers
architectural diagrams.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.880
And it's-- Urs is
one of the authors.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:34.910
It's a cool piece.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:36.920
There's a blog as
well as a white paper.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:38.727
So if anyone wants
to nerd out, I

00:05:38.727 --> 00:05:40.060
think it's a really great paper.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:41.476
And it's worth
reading so you have

00:05:41.476 --> 00:05:44.010
an understanding of how are
we able to get the throughput

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.130
that we do.

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:49.630
The VMs that we have are
running now 16 gig sustained.

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:52.050
So it's not like a
10 GigE connection

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:55.830
with TCP over IP where you're
losing 20% to 30% overhead.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.015
When we say it's a 16 gig
sustained, we really mean that.

00:05:59.015 --> 00:06:03.070
The machines have an excess
of 40 gig throughput per box.

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:05.830
So in terms of
Cloud file systems,

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.200
some customers are able
to mount NFS directly.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.080
We've announced that we have
Compute opening up in Oregon.

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.630
And the cool part
with that is you'll

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:17.500
be just over 20 milliseconds
away from there here in LA.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.610
So West Coast presence,
really excited about that.

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:23.582
That's kind of a late June,
early availability July.

00:06:23.582 --> 00:06:25.540
The facility has been up
and running for years,

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:26.920
so it's not a new build.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:28.630
It's just we're carving
off a chunk of it

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:31.370
and making that available.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:33.794
Most of our customers end up
putting a Cloud file system.

00:06:33.794 --> 00:06:35.210
Even customers in
London where you

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:38.220
can throw a rock to Belgium, and
it's a seven millisecond ping,

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:41.720
you're in a scenario where
bandwidth becomes expensive.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:45.380
And you're reading all the data
at least twice for motion blur,

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:46.420
as you know.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:49.130
So in that case, multiple
reads, cash hits in the Cloud

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:50.230
are friendly.

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:52.420
There are a variety
of solutions.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.910
We support managed solutions
such as Gluster from Red Hat.

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:59.810
We also support read
through cache file systems,

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:01.679
Avere, PixCache as well.

00:07:01.679 --> 00:07:03.720
The advantage of a read
through cache file system

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:05.510
is the fact that
you can deploy it,

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.605
and you have zero
changes to your pipeline,

00:07:07.605 --> 00:07:08.980
initially, to get
up and running.

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:11.190
You can make a picture
without modifying anything.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:13.574
The file system will
immediately look

00:07:13.574 --> 00:07:14.740
to see if it's in the cache.

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:16.860
If it's not there, it will go
to the core file or on-prem

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:17.651
and get it for you.

00:07:17.651 --> 00:07:19.740
We'll talk a little more
about that architecture.

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:22.660
So this is just a straight-up
Cloud file system.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:25.550
In this context, you could
deploy our no cost, single mode

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:26.630
filer.

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.399
And that is a framework
that we have different file

00:07:29.399 --> 00:07:30.690
systems that run underneath it.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:33.130
But for you, it's a
click to deploy option.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:35.129
So you get the same
16 gig throughput,

00:07:35.129 --> 00:07:36.670
you get a couple
hundred gigs of RAM,

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:40.540
you get terabytes of
local SSD backing you.

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:43.190
And then you can put
between 10 and 64 terabytes

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:44.036
of persistent SSD.

00:07:44.036 --> 00:07:45.410
I'll talk more
about the storage,

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:47.520
but it's a pretty
beefy little server.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.270
It's not intended to
be high availability.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:50.090
It's a single mode.

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:52.900
And it's not fault
tolerant in that regard.

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:56.120
But it works really well
for 50 to 100 nodes.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:57.960
If you want to
scale beyond that,

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.840
Avere is here today to talk
with you about their offering.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.360
The Virtual Ethics T is
a fantastic solution.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.020
What's cool about it
is in this example,

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:08.410
you have the Avere
on-prem, but you also

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:10.490
have a virtual version
of it in the Cloud.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:12.880
Now, most of you who
have an Avere product

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:15.520
are used to it being a
multi-terabyte box that's

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:17.120
mounted in your data center.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.209
It fronts your core
filer for you on-prem.

00:08:19.209 --> 00:08:20.750
The difference with
the cloud version

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:24.000
is that each node has
multiple terabytes on it.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:27.800
You can have three to start
with, and go to 10, 20, 30.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:31.100
We've had customers
running 60,000 plus cores

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:34.214
against 18 to 20 Avere
nodes, and the Avere's

00:08:34.214 --> 00:08:35.270
not breaking a sweat.

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:38.330
So you can scale up
beyond 50 if you choose.

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:40.350
So that really changes
how you think about it.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:44.450
The on-prem appliances,
again, a smaller front end

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:46.600
cache where this is
an entire file system.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:48.740
It's your core
filer in the Cloud.

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:51.960
This is a slightly more
advanced diagram here

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.890
which, really, the
point of this is

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:57.800
to try and show you have
on-premises storage as well

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.840
as Cloud, and just to show the
cross connects that you have.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.710
This enables you to
run hybrid rendering.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:04.530
So in this scenario,
you can have

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.680
artists submit to their
local workstation,

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:10.270
to the rack over in the corner,
your colo up the street,

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:11.330
and to the Cloud.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:13.667
You can use a queuing system
that will cut your image up

00:09:13.667 --> 00:09:15.250
into a whole bunch
of different tiles,

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:16.695
and you can
distribute it over all

00:09:16.695 --> 00:09:18.220
of those different machines.

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.680
And that's really exciting.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:22.570
The local machines
will, of course,

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:23.750
read off the local Avere.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:27.220
And the Cloud machines
will read off the VFXT.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:29.090
And Avere's going
to jump on after I'm

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:31.550
up and chat a little
more about their product

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:33.500
and also give you a
demo of the interface.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.690
So from a storage option
standpoint, the primary-- when

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:40.640
you think of Google Cloud
Storage, it's a big blockstore,

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:42.910
and that's not very friendly
for visual effects work.

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:45.750
But it's huge, even exabytes
of data are available on it

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:46.620
currently.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:48.200
The nice part about
it, though, is

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:50.630
that you can store files
there really inexpensively.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:54.400
It's 2.6 cents a gig for
the standard Google Cloud

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:55.535
Storage, the GCS offering.

00:09:55.535 --> 00:09:56.910
Most visual effects
customers who

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:58.410
are parking data
there, though, will

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:00.320
use the durable
reduced availability.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:02.095
It's kind of a
strangely named product.

00:10:02.095 --> 00:10:03.910
But what it is,
it's $0.02 a gig,

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:07.010
and it enables you to read the
data as frequently as you want.

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:09.450
So for production data, you
want us to run the Cloud,

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:10.910
DRA is a really great solution.

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:14.660
You're not performing
BigQuery and large data

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:16.840
analytic workloads on
it, so you don't need

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:19.210
the super low latency of GCS.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:22.905
The slightly less availability
but still durable of DRA

00:10:22.905 --> 00:10:23.409
is great.

00:10:23.409 --> 00:10:25.950
Nearline's a cool product I'll
talk about a little bit later.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:27.280
It's $0.01 a gig.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:31.730
It's our competitor to,
basically, LTO tape solutions.

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:34.900
It's two to seven
seconds to retrieve it.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.140
Persistent disk, that
is our word for network

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:39.200
attached storage.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:41.430
So the standard
is spinning disks.

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:44.880
Most everyone in this industry
is using our distributed SSD

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.060
variant of that.

00:10:46.060 --> 00:10:48.720
You can do cool things
with it, like take 50 nodes

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.780
and mount the PD SSD
read only against them,

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:54.080
and there's your
inexpensive file system.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.610
So it's cool technology.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:58.450
The last item is local SSD.

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:01.317
And that's the one I really
enjoy working with the most,

00:11:01.317 --> 00:11:03.400
just in the fact that it's
super high performance.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.080
For nerds in the
room, right here,

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:08.880
it's 700,000 IOPS
which is really fast.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:11.280
It's basically the speed
of DDR two speed RAM.

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:14.046
And we currently have it
deployed, four disks, striped,

00:11:14.046 --> 00:11:15.420
all that's taken
care of for you.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:16.720
It's three terabytes in size.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:19.222
You can go 750
chunks up to three.

00:11:19.222 --> 00:11:20.680
And of course,
expect that to go up

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:23.082
in terms of performance
and size over time.

00:11:23.082 --> 00:11:24.790
But the cool part
about that is if you're

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:26.950
doing caching operations
or simulation work,

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:28.810
it's really great for IO.

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:30.860
So from a transfer
standpoint, we

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:33.380
have all-- these are just
suggestions for various tools.

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:37.550
You use the gsutil to be able
to communicate with the Cloud

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:39.030
cluster that you've put up.

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.580
And then everything from
rsync all the way down to FDT

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:45.225
to commercial offerings
like Aspera and Signiant

00:11:45.225 --> 00:11:48.110
are supported.

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:50.449
So asset management, one
of the exciting things--

00:11:50.449 --> 00:11:52.490
I don't have a clock, so
I'm just going to check.

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:53.050
There we go.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:56.750
One of the exciting things about
how to optimize the solution

00:11:56.750 --> 00:12:00.160
is looking at your asset
management pipeline.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:03.590
So who here uses
Shotgun in the room?

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:04.210
Show of hands.

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:05.260
Cool.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:08.360
Who has their own proprietary
asset management system

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:11.180
that they roll themselves?

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:12.260
OK, great show of hands.

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:12.860
Cool.

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:15.440
So everybody here knows
that if you just throw up

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.750
a read through cache file
system, such as the Avere,

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:19.350
and you spin up a whole
bunch of render nodes,

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:21.558
you're still going to have
to transfer everything up.

00:12:21.558 --> 00:12:23.490
We've had really good
success with customers

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:26.640
who are doing something
as simple as adding

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:27.700
a single rsync.

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:29.380
So let's say you're
using Alembic.

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:30.756
You cache out the
animator scene.

00:12:30.756 --> 00:12:32.421
You do a quick render
of it to make sure

00:12:32.421 --> 00:12:33.460
that the bake was good.

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:35.800
And you want to scrub
through and RV and see

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:37.537
that there is--
here's the heavy rig

00:12:37.537 --> 00:12:39.120
that you did the
play blast on, here's

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:40.536
the cached version of that.

00:12:40.536 --> 00:12:41.410
Scrub back and forth.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:42.630
The animator goes,
yes, it's good to go.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:44.004
Punch the button,
and publish it.

00:12:44.004 --> 00:12:47.640
At that point, we have people
insert a single rsync line.

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:50.207
That cache file gets
pushed up to the Cloud.

00:12:50.207 --> 00:12:51.790
If you over provision
your file system

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:53.100
just a tiny bit on
the Cloud, you're

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:53.895
in a scenario
where there's going

00:12:53.895 --> 00:12:55.200
to be very little flushing.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:57.033
And it's going to
persistent, so by the time

00:12:57.033 --> 00:12:59.040
it gets down to lighting,
all you're sending up

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.650
is shader values
and light positions,

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:03.400
because you already
have your textures up,

00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:05.660
the character's already
up, the cache files are up.

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:07.359
Everything's been
pushed up in advance.

00:13:07.359 --> 00:13:09.150
You don't care about
performance with rsync

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:11.484
because it's human time between
when the animator clicks

00:13:11.484 --> 00:13:13.650
the button and when production
assigns it to someone

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:14.790
in lighting to pick it up.

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:16.576
The files are already there.

00:13:16.576 --> 00:13:18.700
We also encourage people,
just from an optimization

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:22.820
standpoint, to cache, bake
their files in the Cloud.

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:25.890
And again, uploading in
advance is really optimal.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:28.600
Because our ingress
is free-- I would

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:31.700
agree that there is a cost
to ingress and egress.

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:34.630
But the way it's been
priced is ingress is free,

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:35.980
and egress isn't, for us.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:37.442
So in that context,
you could push

00:13:37.442 --> 00:13:39.400
as much as you want to
the Cloud without having

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:42.300
to pay additional money beyond
using your own balance to do

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:42.940
it.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:44.710
We have some customers
who do-- what

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:47.620
I thought was clever--
was they have a daughter

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:49.910
process that after
their EXRs are finished,

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:51.970
they'll go through and
create an H.264 of that.

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:54.405
And they'll crush it down
from a bitrate standpoint

00:13:54.405 --> 00:13:55.350
of resolution.

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:59.099
And they'll pull just the H.264
down and have a look at it

00:13:59.099 --> 00:14:00.640
and make sure that
the characters are

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:02.950
in the right place, the
lighting is correct,

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:06.680
the paint stuck, and all that
like yes, the render is good.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.430
So then they'll actually
download the EXRs

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:11.440
at that point, which
are 4K and 50 AOVs

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:13.115
and the usual thing, half float.

00:14:13.115 --> 00:14:15.490
So in that context, you're in
a scenario where you're not

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:17.114
paying egress something
that you're not

00:14:17.114 --> 00:14:19.879
sure is a good
render, so to speak.

00:14:19.879 --> 00:14:21.420
So from a queuing
standpoint, we have

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:23.060
a variety of queueing partners.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:25.660
We have representatives
from PipelineFX here today

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:27.990
as well as Thinkbox.

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:29.240
We support Tractor and RUSH.

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:30.615
We have quite a
few customers who

00:14:30.615 --> 00:14:32.002
run their own queuing system.

00:14:32.002 --> 00:14:34.460
So it's a really hard problem,
as everyone here in the room

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:37.640
knows, and we've been working
with all the providers

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:39.310
of the top queuing
systems to ensure

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:42.750
that their queueing system
works well on our cloud,

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:45.990
it can communicate on-prem
with an existing control head,

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:46.690
back and forth.

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:49.326
So there's varying levels
of cloud integration

00:14:49.326 --> 00:14:51.450
with these products but
would happily talk with you

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:52.890
after about more information.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:54.889
And again, we have reps
from those two companies

00:14:54.889 --> 00:14:56.630
here today to talk with you.

00:14:56.630 --> 00:14:59.080
So from a licensing
standpoint-- yeah,

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:01.670
when I joined the company about
18 months ago, the group of us

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:03.420
sat down and made a
list of all the things

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:05.129
we needed from a VFX
standpoint, in order

00:15:05.129 --> 00:15:06.544
to be able to
deploy in the Cloud.

00:15:06.544 --> 00:15:08.900
And like top of the list,
right after Cloud File System,

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:09.940
was Cloud Licensing.

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:11.840
And with permanent
billing, you want

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:15.430
to make sure you can also
render in the same capacity.

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:18.260
It's difficult for me to
get up here and say, hey,

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:19.350
you have 1,200 frame shot.

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:21.160
Let's spin up 1,200 machines.

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:25.030
It's 17 to 24 minutes or two
hours of frame or 2.1 hours,

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:27.822
and you only want to pay for
the 2.1, not round up to three.

00:15:27.822 --> 00:15:29.530
The immediate response
I get back on that

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:30.620
is, what about licensing.

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:35.385
So today, Chris Nichols
is here from Chaos Group.

00:15:35.385 --> 00:15:36.760
And he can talk
with you a little

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:40.160
after about their Cloud
Licensing solution.

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:41.800
So you can do
permanent licensing

00:15:41.800 --> 00:15:43.870
on our Cloud that's
fully dynamic.

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:47.311
Deadline from Thinkbox has
Krakatoa support, as well as

00:15:47.311 --> 00:15:49.810
NUKE from the Foundry, pretty
much the whole Foundry product

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:53.340
line, including VRay, as
well, from Chaos Group.

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:56.900
And Qube from Pipeline
FX has currently

00:15:56.900 --> 00:16:00.310
beta support for cloud licensing
for their queuing system.

00:16:00.310 --> 00:16:02.880
So if you're a Qube user, you'll
be able to scale dynamically

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.067
on the cloud as well.

00:16:04.067 --> 00:16:05.650
If you're Qube and
VRay, for instance,

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:08.980
you could point at Chaos Group
server as well as the Pipeline

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:09.880
FX server.

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:11.550
So these are really
great solutions

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:14.470
to enable you to be able to
say, hey, I'm a smaller studio.

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:17.130
But I want to compete
with the large studios,

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:19.540
because, as you know,
10 machines for 10 hours

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:21.640
cost the same as 100
machines for one hour.

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:23.890
But there's that nice, nine
hour wall clock difference

00:16:23.890 --> 00:16:25.370
that you're saving.

00:16:25.370 --> 00:16:28.740
In terms of virtual machines,
it's all the same hardware.

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:31.670
The racks that I showed you
earlier on the Jupiter fabric

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:34.880
page, that's the same
infrastructure that all

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:36.190
of Google services running on.

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:38.842
So Maps, Search, all the
machine learning technology,

00:16:38.842 --> 00:16:40.800
everything runs across
the same infrastructure.

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:43.240
We pretty much have
Compute and Storage.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:46.629
So this is not like a special,
roll your own version for GCP.

00:16:46.629 --> 00:16:48.170
This is the industrial
strength stuff

00:16:48.170 --> 00:16:49.980
that Google runs everything on.

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.310
And because of that, we
have live migration support,

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:54.290
and we're the only
company that has this.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:57.320
It's the ability to,
basically, snapshot a machine

00:16:57.320 --> 00:16:59.980
and move it to another
box on another rack

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:01.480
without interrupting the job.

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:03.500
The live migration even
supports use cases,

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:06.349
such as a file system
that's under heavy load

00:17:06.349 --> 00:17:07.329
with local SSD.

00:17:07.329 --> 00:17:10.744
We can migrate that file
system while it's running.

00:17:10.744 --> 00:17:12.410
And it'll take several
minutes to do it,

00:17:12.410 --> 00:17:14.869
but we can actually move it
without interrupting the job.

00:17:14.869 --> 00:17:17.159
That gives you huge
amounts of uptime.

00:17:17.159 --> 00:17:18.950
That means that we can
fail a power supply,

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:20.408
we can have hard
drives go down, we

00:17:20.408 --> 00:17:22.740
can have all sorts
of the usual problems

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.420
that one has in a
very large scale data

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:26.380
center without
interrupting the workflow.

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:29.190
So that's a huge advantage
with standard VMs.

00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:31.470
The other nice thing is,
from a pricing standpoint,

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:32.870
there's a sustained
use discount.

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:35.170
So if you want to use the
machine five days a week,

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:36.756
but you want to turn
it off on Friday

00:17:36.756 --> 00:17:38.380
night through Sunday
afternoon, however

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:40.810
you want to use it,
you only pay the lowest

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:42.060
price at the end of the month.

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:45.080
So the maximum discount is
30% off our standard pricing.

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:48.990
However, if you use it
21 days of the 30 or 31,

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:51.130
you're getting kind
of a 22% to 25%.

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:54.830
So you don't have to
buy in advance or budget

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:55.520
in that regard.

00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:58.026
You just pay the lowest price
at the end of the month.

00:17:58.026 --> 00:17:59.650
Most of our customers
who are rendering

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:01.830
are taking advantage
of preemptible VMs.

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:03.440
These are the same
VMs that you're

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:05.830
running standard
jobs on, except this

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:08.670
is excess capacity for Google.

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:11.030
Unlike a rendering data
center, where you guys

00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:13.970
are running at like 98%
efficiency-- well, 98%

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:16.410
compute usage, I should
say, at all times.

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:18.720
Google runs with higher
margins than that.

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.250
And that enables Search
to be highly responsive

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:23.450
and take significant loads.

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:27.890
The scenario for
preemptible VMs, 24 hour

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.000
lease, they're 70% off.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.740
So you're paying
$0.30 on the dollar.

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:36.410
To give you an example, that's
32 cores with 120 gigs of RAM,

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:40.130
stood up, including network
and power and spares

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:41.770
is $0.48 an hour.

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:43.270
So that's really inexpensive.

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:47.050
When you do the math on what
the total cost of ownership is,

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:50.230
we're pretty confident that's
a really competitive price.

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:52.420
The machines do get
taken back on occasion

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:54.650
if we need capacity,
or if we need to have

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:55.740
that rack for some reason.

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:58.026
You'll get a 30 second
termination on it.

00:18:58.026 --> 00:18:59.650
Integration with your
queuing software,

00:18:59.650 --> 00:19:02.390
we'll talk a little more
about how to handle that.

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:03.720
So VM strategies.

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:06.480
So standard VMs, you want to
use that for your gateways,

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:07.960
your Cloud File Systems.

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:10.020
If you have a render
that's more than 24 hours,

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:11.900
please put it on a standard VM.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:17.097
Do consider use checkpointing
with VRay or PRman or Arnold.

00:19:17.097 --> 00:19:18.680
Critical deliveries,
if you absolutely

00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:20.700
have to have it tomorrow
at a specific time,

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:23.080
and you don't want to have
the job get interrupted,

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:26.000
potentially, and then
r-spin up a new machine

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:27.590
and start from your
last checkpoint,

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:29.720
there may be a couple--
maybe tens of minutes

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:31.178
or more between
the last checkpoint

00:19:31.178 --> 00:19:32.700
and where the machine start.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:35.760
Preemptible VMs, however,
are fantastic for doing

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:39.677
render's normal work, new comps,
any of that work is fantastic.

00:19:39.677 --> 00:19:41.260
I would also encourage
simulation work

00:19:41.260 --> 00:19:43.120
to run on standards,
if it's something

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:45.900
that's important to you
in the long running.

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:48.810
Making your queuing software
aware of preemptible VMs.

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:52.380
We have a pub/sub service that
basically looks at the Cloud

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:55.840
log, and you can basically
point your queueing system

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:57.790
at that to look
at whether or not

00:19:57.790 --> 00:19:59.130
a machine is going to go down.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:02.320
If that occurs, we
encourage the scripting

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:05.550
to be such that you send a
termination to the machine

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:06.660
of the job you're running.

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:08.520
So if you can get a
checkpoint out of it, great.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:10.000
You have 30 seconds
to write the data,

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.083
and you're pushing it back
to a cloud file system.

00:20:12.083 --> 00:20:13.440
It's usually successful.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:16.194
You also immediately stop
sending jobs to that machine

00:20:16.194 --> 00:20:17.110
if you're bin packing.

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:18.380
So if you have a
32 core machine,

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:20.296
and you're treating it
like an on-prem rental,

00:20:20.296 --> 00:20:22.220
you may want to be running
16 cores for render

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:23.971
and some for comp and
some for other jobs.

00:20:23.971 --> 00:20:26.136
If that's the case, you
want to stop submitting jobs

00:20:26.136 --> 00:20:26.920
to that machine.

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:29.120
You can immediately
spin up another machine.

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:30.662
It's just that we
want that one back.

00:20:30.662 --> 00:20:32.078
But we still have
stock elsewhere.

00:20:32.078 --> 00:20:33.630
So you get another
machine coming up,

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:35.970
which takes about 45 seconds
to get the machine up,

00:20:35.970 --> 00:20:39.090
and then you can start putting
your render executable on it

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:40.240
and loading data into it.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:42.120
So again, as one
machine goes down,

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:43.230
the next machine comes up.

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:46.370
And you can get pixels back
in roughly under two minutes,

00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:48.910
depending how large
your scene is to load.

00:20:48.910 --> 00:20:52.146
So in terms of different
workloads that we have,

00:20:52.146 --> 00:20:54.160
we're having great
success with people

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:55.630
running rendering workloads.

00:20:55.630 --> 00:20:58.090
But we have other customers
who have come to us and said,

00:20:58.090 --> 00:20:59.710
hey, we've got a
big effect show,

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:01.250
and it's 10x what we have.

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:04.300
And I'm sure
everyone in this room

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:08.490
has had issues with high
I/O on the local network

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:11.030
where even if you have a
physical caching solution

00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:12.760
on-prem, you're
still in a scenario

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:16.300
where-- an example
I can give, you

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:18.700
have hundreds of fluid sims
running at the same time.

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:22.310
I had that on "All You Need is
Kill" was the name of the film.

00:21:22.310 --> 00:21:25.840
And "Edge of Tomorrow" was
the final release name.

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:27.360
That final shot
in the Thames, we

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:30.520
had hundreds of naiad fluid
sims going simultaneously.

00:21:30.520 --> 00:21:33.340
And it was just crushing
the I/O on the file system.

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:36.250
The caching appliance was
being forced to flush,

00:21:36.250 --> 00:21:37.960
because we were
reading huge data sets

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:41.510
and writing 20 to 30 or
more gigs per frame out.

00:21:41.510 --> 00:21:43.700
And as a result, we needed
to segment the network

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:45.510
and use a different file system.

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:47.430
So the opportunity to
do that on the Cloud

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:50.740
is significant in the fact
that our network is phenomenal.

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:54.500
And the file system
performance is extremely high.

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:58.410
Compositing, decompositing,
I know the standard workloads

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:01.620
are, it takes too long
to push up normal comps

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:04.130
and then do the minor
compute that's required

00:22:04.130 --> 00:22:05.400
and then pull the data down.

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:07.330
However, for decompositing,
because the I/O

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:09.640
is so significant,
we encourage people

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.850
to use the Cloud for that,
in the fact that it's really

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:14.730
providing a significant
benefit by not

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:16.300
crushing their local renders.

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:19.480
And for archive use
cases, the ability

00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:23.240
to store the data on the Cloud
and have your entire tool

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:25.072
chain from the
previous file, I'm

00:22:25.072 --> 00:22:26.905
sure everyone here is
running multiple shows

00:22:26.905 --> 00:22:29.100
in their facility,
has disparate versions

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:30.275
of environments and such.

00:22:30.275 --> 00:22:32.430
And if you want to
go back and say,

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:35.750
I want to restore that robot
from the previous movie,

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:38.100
but I also need to restore
the entire environment

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:38.910
that it was in.

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:40.780
You can have a snapshot
of that tool chain,

00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:42.640
you can have the asset
available to you,

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:44.490
you can get it in
two to seven seconds,

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:46.450
as opposed to a
multi-hour restore

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:50.270
if you have on-prem for LTO,
as well as a multi-day restore

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:52.520
if that file is elsewhere.

00:22:52.520 --> 00:22:54.740
So the advantage of
this is it gives you

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:57.170
the ability to let the
artist work immediately.

00:22:57.170 --> 00:22:59.580
You can pull just the
files off you want.

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:01.820
You don't have to restore
entire directories.

00:23:01.820 --> 00:23:04.610
And you're in a scenario
where, instead of saying,

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:06.324
I need to pull back
20 gigs of something

00:23:06.324 --> 00:23:08.490
and realize it was actually
stored in someone's home

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:10.573
directory, you can actually
traverse the file tree

00:23:10.573 --> 00:23:12.350
and get just the files you want.

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:14.230
And it's only a penny per gig.

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:17.257
You can also upload
a 400 terabyte show

00:23:17.257 --> 00:23:19.840
because you have to get it off
your local file system on-prem.

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:21.400
But then if you have a
little bit of downtime,

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:23.500
you can put a talented
Pipe TA on to the job

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:26.170
and go through and pare
that show down and get it

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:31.160
down to 225, 175 gigs, whatever
you can get that show down to.

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:33.160
Once you've already shipped
it, and it's already

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:35.510
been shown in the theaters, you
can get that minimum archive.

00:23:35.510 --> 00:23:36.890
That's hard to do without LTO.

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:39.840
I don't know anyone who's
actually onlined LTO tapes

00:23:39.840 --> 00:23:43.260
and then pared them down
before putting them back out.

00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:46.220
So the rental model is
what people started with.

00:23:46.220 --> 00:23:49.300
The idea was, Bob and
Sarah come by with a truck

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:51.079
and rack up three more racks.

00:23:51.079 --> 00:23:53.370
And you plug them in, you
have enough network capacity,

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:56.930
you have cooling and storage,
and physical foot space

00:23:56.930 --> 00:23:58.780
for the boxes to sit there.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:23:59.730
That's fine.

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:01.927
Your queuing system picks
them up and away you go.

00:24:01.927 --> 00:24:03.510
The problem with
that model is that it

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:05.690
doesn't scale as well
as you might like it to.

00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:08.080
And in some markets, it's
difficult from a power

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:08.630
standpoint.

00:24:08.630 --> 00:24:11.210
It's expensive to get
power here in California.

00:24:11.210 --> 00:24:13.090
People often move
to a colo model,

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:15.070
so they can have a
larger physical space

00:24:15.070 --> 00:24:17.350
and make better use of
their on-prem facility

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:19.740
to put more artists and such.

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:22.700
Companies in London where
it's super expensive,

00:24:22.700 --> 00:24:25.320
they're out of cooling,
and power is a problem.

00:24:25.320 --> 00:24:27.500
The problem with this
is it's fixed scale.

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:29.630
The dynamic model is the
one I'm most excited about

00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:31.930
and the fact that this is
the wall clock savings.

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:34.670
So if you run a significant
number of machines

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:36.420
for a short period
of time, because we

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:38.655
have permanent billing
after the first 10 minutes,

00:24:38.655 --> 00:24:40.280
the cost is going to
be the same to you

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:43.310
if you run 1,000 machines
in a 24 hour period

00:24:43.310 --> 00:24:46.230
as if you run 4,000 machines
in a six hour period.

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:48.085
But the difference
is is that the comp

00:24:48.085 --> 00:24:50.127
is able to work on
that file tonight.

00:24:50.127 --> 00:24:52.460
And the lighting artist doesn't
have to come in tomorrow

00:24:52.460 --> 00:24:54.420
and say, yeah,
it's on [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:54.420 --> 00:24:55.810
but it's still on the farm.

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:58.160
You never have it
still on the farm.

00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:00.230
You're able to get
the shots through.

00:25:00.230 --> 00:25:04.170
And when you save a couple of
days of production time, that's

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:06.280
the effects time, that's
producer time, all

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:08.400
the different soups, leads.

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:11.649
That's a significant per
day cost that you're saving.

00:25:11.649 --> 00:25:13.940
Here are some of the customers
that we're working with.

00:25:13.940 --> 00:25:15.400
Some of them are local.

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:18.880
Some of them are
global in presence.

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:21.290
Framestore had a really
great success with us.

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:24.030
They were one of our
earlier customers.

00:25:24.030 --> 00:25:28.840
They workee-- well, they put a
really great system, I think.

00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:31.200
They were running between
6,000 and 12,000 cores.

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:34.700
And they were one of our early
of year launch customers,

00:25:34.700 --> 00:25:35.300
as well.

00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:39.040
Tristan, from Framestore,
participated in a webinar

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:39.740
last fall.

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:43.400
And he talked about being at
roughly 2.6 gig, sustained

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:45.400
throughput on a 3 gig
link from SOHO net

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:47.446
that could scale to 10.

00:25:47.446 --> 00:25:49.070
Once they put the
Avere cache in place,

00:25:49.070 --> 00:25:51.620
they were down about
1.1 to 1.2 gig.

00:25:51.620 --> 00:25:53.840
So that would enable
them to either double

00:25:53.840 --> 00:25:56.500
the number of cores
on a fixed pipe size

00:25:56.500 --> 00:26:01.820
or be able to work with
a significant reduction

00:26:01.820 --> 00:26:03.340
in throughput, as
well, and still

00:26:03.340 --> 00:26:05.817
maintain the same
number of cores.

00:26:05.817 --> 00:26:07.900
So one of things I just
want to talk briefly about

00:26:07.900 --> 00:26:09.380
is the total cost of ownership.

00:26:09.380 --> 00:26:14.490
And for us, I have performance
on one side and cost over time.

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:17.500
And this example is just
a 36 month amortization.

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:19.810
So the on-premise is, let's
say, for fun, you go out

00:26:19.810 --> 00:26:22.524
and you buy 5,000 cores on-prem.

00:26:22.524 --> 00:26:24.190
The performance is
going to be the same.

00:26:24.190 --> 00:26:26.890
They will render the same
amount over that period of time.

00:26:26.890 --> 00:26:29.410
So by the 36th month,
if you're paying

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:31.470
for it per month, the
cost for the cores

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:34.970
are the same, your per monthly
cost if you financed it.

00:26:34.970 --> 00:26:36.680
And the performance is the same.

00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:38.840
With Google, our prices go down.

00:26:38.840 --> 00:26:40.520
We have a Moore's
law of pricing.

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:44.410
So we're doing reductions on
a more frequent basis than 18

00:26:44.410 --> 00:26:47.220
months, but we're pretty much
doing the half every 18 months.

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:49.790
So the price will be
significantly less three years

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:51.190
in the future than it is today.

00:26:51.190 --> 00:26:52.910
But the cool thing
is, is that you

00:26:52.910 --> 00:26:55.890
get the architectural
increases over time.

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:58.760
So you'd go from Sandy
Bridge, Ivy Bridge,

00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:02.140
you're doing to Haswell, all
the way to Skylake, Ice Lake.

00:27:02.140 --> 00:27:03.870
You can look at the
future architectures.

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:07.040
So on this graph, you can see
that your performance is going

00:27:07.040 --> 00:27:09.580
to go up several
architectures worth,

00:27:09.580 --> 00:27:11.211
and your cost is
going to be down.

00:27:11.211 --> 00:27:12.710
And the nice thing
is is you're only

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:14.420
paying for this for the
minutes you're using it.

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:16.174
If you have a slowdown
in production,

00:27:16.174 --> 00:27:17.590
if you go back
into story, if they

00:27:17.590 --> 00:27:21.000
want to shoot pick ups, whatever
that is, you can shut it off.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:23.010
If you've prepaid for
compute, then you're

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:25.400
in a story where you're just
paying for it whether you're

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:26.364
using it or not.

00:27:26.364 --> 00:27:28.030
And that's a completely
different model.

00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:31.780
So we're getting really
good adoption with this.

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:33.760
So Production Safety Valve.

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:36.520
This is the idea that
we work with customers,

00:27:36.520 --> 00:27:40.060
and we get a proof of concept in
before the project gets crazy.

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:42.210
I have had calls on a
Friday afternoon saying,

00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:43.540
we're delivering Monday.

00:27:43.540 --> 00:27:44.770
We're crushed.

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:45.560
Can you help us?

00:27:45.560 --> 00:27:47.143
And then you scramble
over the weekend

00:27:47.143 --> 00:27:48.660
to try and set something up.

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:49.750
We can often do that.

00:27:49.750 --> 00:27:51.260
It depends on the complexities.

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:54.350
It's so much nicer when
you're involved in advance.

00:27:54.350 --> 00:27:56.540
The account managers have
some credits available so

00:27:56.540 --> 00:27:57.800
that people can start.

00:27:57.800 --> 00:27:59.870
And most importantly,
it's all about being

00:27:59.870 --> 00:28:01.310
able to make a single picture.

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:04.800
As long as you can get a gateway
set up, get your VPN set up,

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:06.980
and you can either do
an NFS mount, depending

00:28:06.980 --> 00:28:07.980
on where you're located.

00:28:07.980 --> 00:28:10.510
For here, we encourage
a Cloud File System.

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:12.360
You can go with just
a simple single load

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:14.790
filer or a persistent
disk to do a quick test.

00:28:14.790 --> 00:28:17.940
You upload a self-contained
scene, you can render a frame.

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:20.389
We do encourage
everybody to identify

00:28:20.389 --> 00:28:21.680
the machines they have on-prem.

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:24.000
So I'm sure
everybody in the room

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:25.757
has four or five
generations in their data

00:28:25.757 --> 00:28:27.340
center of different
machines that they

00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:29.340
have on-prem plus a couple
different generations

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:32.585
of workstations, depending on
what artists are using them.

00:28:32.585 --> 00:28:33.960
I encourage people
to go and take

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:35.418
a scene that's
self-contained so we

00:28:35.418 --> 00:28:37.810
can get network network
performance for storage

00:28:37.810 --> 00:28:38.720
out of the game.

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:40.550
Put the local file
on the machine.

00:28:40.550 --> 00:28:41.410
Do a render of it.

00:28:41.410 --> 00:28:43.990
If it takes 20 minutes
or an hour, that's fine.

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:45.070
Put a weight on that.

00:28:45.070 --> 00:28:46.780
And put that in a
spreadsheet and say,

00:28:46.780 --> 00:28:48.367
the middle machine was a 1.0.

00:28:48.367 --> 00:28:50.700
Run them on your fast machines
and your slower machines,

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:51.950
and come back with a number.

00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:54.750
Then benchmark all
the cloud providers.

00:28:54.750 --> 00:28:55.766
Benchmark Google.

00:28:55.766 --> 00:28:57.890
And go through and try our
different architectures.

00:28:57.890 --> 00:28:59.306
We currently have
three available,

00:28:59.306 --> 00:29:00.560
Sandy Bridge, Ivy Bridge.

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:01.910
We have Haswell.

00:29:01.910 --> 00:29:04.930
You can imagine Broadwell
may be coming soon.

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:09.210
In that context,
the ability to say,

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:12.200
this job will take 55
minutes on our bare metal,

00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:13.960
and it'll take this
long on the Cloud.

00:29:13.960 --> 00:29:16.060
That really helps from
a scheduling standpoint

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:17.241
and from a cost standpoint.

00:29:17.241 --> 00:29:18.740
Otherwise, it's
like, well, hold on.

00:29:18.740 --> 00:29:21.860
That machine, the 32
[INAUDIBLE] box, $0.48 an hour,

00:29:21.860 --> 00:29:23.480
but how does that equate?

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:27.300
That also enables you to put
a cost on your local render

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:30.075
and say, this machine under
the artist workstation, that

00:29:30.075 --> 00:29:31.310
has a Quadro 6,000 in it.

00:29:31.310 --> 00:29:34.410
That's not an in-- it's a great
card, but it's not inexpensive.

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:35.660
And there's tons of cores.

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:37.660
That box takes up physical
space and requires

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:39.920
spares and s-cooling, and
you guys know the drill.

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:43.760
The opportunity to be able to
say to production, your render

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:45.890
cost tonight is going
to be this much.

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:48.130
Even though you're using
our on-prem, it's not free.

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:49.879
I know it's rolled
into the facility cost.

00:29:49.879 --> 00:29:52.490
But you can start attributing
it to shows so that they say,

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:53.090
fine.

00:29:53.090 --> 00:29:54.820
We're going to give up
some of this for local.

00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:55.800
Or we're going to borrow this.

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.716
We're going to take some
cloud compute and get

00:29:57.716 --> 00:29:58.910
the shot done more quickly.

00:29:58.910 --> 00:30:00.460
The ability to be
able to, again,

00:30:00.460 --> 00:30:02.810
expand to other types of
workflow such as simulation

00:30:02.810 --> 00:30:06.040
and comp, really exciting
because of the file I/O savings

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:08.560
that you get on that.

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:09.840
So please, start early.

00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:11.880
We're happy to help
you get set up.

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:16.849
For those who do transcoding,
we have the fantastic partners

00:30:16.849 --> 00:30:17.390
we have here.

00:30:17.390 --> 00:30:19.420
We also have FFmpeg
which is open source.

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:21.110
YouTube is based on that.

00:30:21.110 --> 00:30:24.625
We have a cool pipeline that you
can use with Qube or Deadline.

00:30:24.625 --> 00:30:26.295
Or you can roll
your own, or we'll

00:30:26.295 --> 00:30:28.880
use Tractor that enables
you to spin up a VM,

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:31.000
copy the file
locally, and then be

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:34.380
able to perform the transcode
using our local SSD,

00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:36.590
and then make 28
different flavors of it

00:30:36.590 --> 00:30:38.830
in different codecs and
different bitrates and such.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:41.910
But today, we actually have
an announcement for you.

00:30:41.910 --> 00:30:43.750
Still in beta, not a
public announcement,

00:30:43.750 --> 00:30:47.010
but for this room, we have
Colorfront's transcoder

00:30:47.010 --> 00:30:48.460
running on our platform.

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:49.670
So it's still in beta.

00:30:49.670 --> 00:30:50.910
It works very well.

00:30:50.910 --> 00:30:52.620
So we have a couple
of initial customers

00:30:52.620 --> 00:30:54.036
we're working with
who are running

00:30:54.036 --> 00:30:55.690
production workloads on it.

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:58.091
But it's a really cool
professional transcoder

00:30:58.091 --> 00:31:00.090
that'll do ProRes, and
everything else you want,

00:31:00.090 --> 00:31:02.120
running on our platform.

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:04.330
