00:32:41.320 --> 00:32:45.320
like running within GC itself.

00:32:45.320 --> 00:32:48.390
So as Jeff mentioned,
we start out typically

00:32:48.390 --> 00:32:50.320
with a three node cluster.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:52.380
And each of these
nodes in Avere node

00:32:52.380 --> 00:32:56.340
is going to be sitting on
a instance type within GCE.

00:32:56.340 --> 00:33:01.290
And today, we support highmen-8
and highmen-32 instance types.

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:04.120
And then we have a couple--
or a few different-- storage

00:33:04.120 --> 00:33:05.360
options.

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:08.960
So on the persistent side,
we'll support either one or four

00:33:08.960 --> 00:33:11.210
terabytes of persistent SSD.

00:33:11.210 --> 00:33:14.160
Or on the local SSD
side, we'll support

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:15.566
one and a half terabytes.

00:33:15.566 --> 00:33:17.660
And that's per instance.

00:33:17.660 --> 00:33:20.840
And again, we start
out with a FXT cluster

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:23.260
that's three nodes in size.

00:33:23.260 --> 00:33:25.990
And then as you need
additional throughput,

00:33:25.990 --> 00:33:29.550
you just add one node at a time
until you get to the desired

00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:32.610
throughput and I/O levels.

00:33:32.610 --> 00:33:36.860
So for a deployment
within GCP, obviously

00:33:36.860 --> 00:33:38.980
you will need a
project to install in.

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:40.715
We'll typically talk to you.

00:33:40.715 --> 00:33:43.090
But make sure that you have--
from a quota point of view,

00:33:43.090 --> 00:33:44.760
if you just opened
up your project,

00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:47.640
you may need to add
some additional quota

00:33:47.640 --> 00:33:50.790
for either persistent
SSD or instance type,

00:33:50.790 --> 00:33:53.876
depending on how many
nodes you're spinning up.

00:33:53.876 --> 00:33:57.680
And then we do have a couple
of infrastructure instances

00:33:57.680 --> 00:33:58.460
that we bring up.

00:33:58.460 --> 00:34:01.370
So the first one is going
to be a NAT instance.

00:34:01.370 --> 00:34:06.550
And we use that to route API
calls and any S3 calls out

00:34:06.550 --> 00:34:08.360
to GCS storage.

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:10.830
So the reason why
we have to have that

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:14.750
is that API is accessible
basically from internet side.

00:34:14.750 --> 00:34:17.710
So within your project
and within your subnet,

00:34:17.710 --> 00:34:20.360
we just need to be able
to issue those API calls.

00:34:20.360 --> 00:34:24.402
And we use those for things
like, when we do HA failovers,

00:34:24.402 --> 00:34:27.060
if we're moving an IP from
one instance to another,

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:32.110
for example, we want to be able
to move that IP transparently

00:34:32.110 --> 00:34:35.131
so that the workload continues
even in the face of an instance

00:34:35.131 --> 00:34:35.630
failure.

00:34:35.630 --> 00:34:38.699
And we can do all that if
we have access to the API.

00:34:38.699 --> 00:34:41.280
Obviously, to create
instances within the cluster,

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:43.670
we would need to
do that as well.

00:34:43.670 --> 00:34:47.219
So the next thing that we do is
we modify the firewall rules.

00:34:47.219 --> 00:34:49.300
We're actually
using an IP address

00:34:49.300 --> 00:34:54.370
space that's typically above
what gets assigned by Google.

00:34:54.370 --> 00:34:56.179
And the reason why
we do that is we're

00:34:56.179 --> 00:34:59.940
using more IPs than the
Google API will typically

00:34:59.940 --> 00:35:02.190
provide for an instance.

00:35:02.190 --> 00:35:04.280
And I'll show you a chart
of what that looks like,

00:35:04.280 --> 00:35:06.060
a little graphic of that.

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:08.174
Then the next step
is we bring up

00:35:08.174 --> 00:35:09.840
what's called the
Cluster Manager, which

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:11.170
is another instance.

00:35:11.170 --> 00:35:13.950
And that gives you the
graphical user interface

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:16.000
for actually
deploying the system.

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:18.200
And I'll show you
that within the demo.

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:20.350
The next step is we
go through-- we use

00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:22.470
that UI to create the cluster.

00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:27.200
Then we basically UI
into the Virtual FXT.

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:29.890
Once it's configured, we
complete the configuration.

00:35:29.890 --> 00:35:33.000
And from that point, you're
ready to mount your clients

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:35.810
and start your
workload against it.

00:35:35.810 --> 00:35:37.230
So if we'll go to
the next slide,

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:41.641
I'll show you what that looks
like from a graphical point

00:35:41.641 --> 00:35:42.140
