I don't know if we want to
listen to that right now.

00:44:14.910 --> 00:44:19.250
But nonetheless, I can take
a look at the ops per second.

00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:22.610
I can also change this
to the throughput.

00:44:22.610 --> 00:44:26.160
And I could also
bring up latency.

00:44:26.160 --> 00:44:28.040
And again, this
is client facing.

00:44:28.040 --> 00:44:31.390
What I also can do is I
can take a look at-- drill

00:44:31.390 --> 00:44:33.100
down into either a
cluster wide view,

00:44:33.100 --> 00:44:36.660
or I can take a
view of the Vserver.

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:39.460
Now what a Vserver is is
just a virtual representation

00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:43.790
of whatever NAS product
you have sitting behind us,

00:44:43.790 --> 00:44:46.860
or whatever object
storage device you have,

00:44:46.860 --> 00:44:51.120
which could be GCP as
well as something on-prem.

00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:56.490
Could be object storage on-prem,
or it could be a NAS on-prem.

00:44:56.490 --> 00:45:00.360
But I can actually define what I
want to look at here, depending

00:45:00.360 --> 00:45:05.280
on what I'm interested in at
that particular point in time.

00:45:05.280 --> 00:45:08.530
I can change this
time for the dashboard

00:45:08.530 --> 00:45:10.110
to be-- my default's
five minutes.

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:12.910
I can, as you can see here, I
can make it whatever I want.

00:45:12.910 --> 00:45:16.880
I could also select a date and
time which can be interesting.

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:19.120
Say I want to look at
something from maybe two, three

00:45:19.120 --> 00:45:22.650
weeks ago, four weeks ago,
this goes back in time

00:45:22.650 --> 00:45:27.740
until you last rebooted the
cluster or did an install.

00:45:27.740 --> 00:45:33.160
So there's a point where it
actually removes some of the--

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:35.080
or writes over some of the data.

00:45:35.080 --> 00:45:38.740
Let's just go put this
back at five minutes.

00:45:38.740 --> 00:45:41.240
The chart controls here
we can take a look at.

00:45:41.240 --> 00:45:44.250
We can change the colors
on the ops per second.

00:45:44.250 --> 00:45:48.446
We have client ops which is,
obviously we know what that is.

00:45:48.446 --> 00:45:49.820
It's the clients
doing operations

00:45:49.820 --> 00:45:51.799
into the Avere cluster.

00:45:51.799 --> 00:45:52.590
This is the source.

00:45:52.590 --> 00:45:57.280
So I could change that
depending on what I have.

00:45:57.280 --> 00:46:01.450
And I can actually change
these colors if I-- for myself,

00:46:01.450 --> 00:46:04.270
I'm slightly color blind, so I
might pick something that where

00:46:04.270 --> 00:46:05.300
I can actually read it.

00:46:05.300 --> 00:46:08.650
But data management
ops is the operations

00:46:08.650 --> 00:46:13.370
that we use for-- we have
a couple features called

00:46:13.370 --> 00:46:15.490
Move and Mirror,
FlashMove and FlashMirror.

00:46:15.490 --> 00:46:18.005
So those are the
data management ops

00:46:18.005 --> 00:46:20.520
that you would see there if
we were actually moving data

00:46:20.520 --> 00:46:23.190
from one particular
core filer-- which,

00:46:23.190 --> 00:46:26.270
again, is the storage
repository-- to another storage

00:46:26.270 --> 00:46:26.970
repository.

00:46:26.970 --> 00:46:30.380
Say you want to move from your
local NAS box up through us

00:46:30.380 --> 00:46:36.400
into an object storage into
GCP, or GCS, pardon me.

00:46:36.400 --> 00:46:37.820
On the bottom of
the dashboard, we

00:46:37.820 --> 00:46:39.240
have a couple
things that we need

00:46:39.240 --> 00:46:41.410
to make note of which
is the conditions.

00:46:41.410 --> 00:46:45.074
So these conditions could
be either red or yellow.

00:46:45.074 --> 00:46:46.490
Red would be,
obviously, an alert,

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:48.050
and you'd want to
definitely take

00:46:48.050 --> 00:46:49.510
a look at that immediately.

00:46:49.510 --> 00:46:51.740
If it's yellow, it's not
too much of a concern.

00:46:51.740 --> 00:46:54.720
The conditions should
usually clear by themselves.

00:46:54.720 --> 00:46:57.940
If they don't, then we want
to take a closer look at it.

00:46:57.940 --> 00:46:59.930
The alerts is just
what it is, alert.

00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:03.040
And what we have here
is-- just before you

00:47:03.040 --> 00:47:05.560
guys came, and I
added the core filer

00:47:05.560 --> 00:47:07.819
which was my Linux NAS box.

00:47:07.819 --> 00:47:09.860
And of course, it gave me
a couple of alerts here

00:47:09.860 --> 00:47:14.710
that-- I had actually removed it
last night when I was testing.

00:47:14.710 --> 00:47:16.870
So the virtual server,
again, the virtual server's

00:47:16.870 --> 00:47:20.530
is Avere's representation
of what these core

00:47:20.530 --> 00:47:23.060
files are sitting behind us.

00:47:23.060 --> 00:47:29.580
We have one Vserver,
which is presenting

00:47:29.580 --> 00:47:31.640
all of the core filers
sitting behind us

00:47:31.640 --> 00:47:33.840
into a global namespace.

00:47:33.840 --> 00:47:37.670
So that means that
each individual export

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:42.160
from the core filers represented
with a junction point.

00:47:42.160 --> 00:47:45.730
And I'll show you that when
we get into the settings.

00:47:45.730 --> 00:47:49.100
The Vserver has a group
of client facing IPs.

00:47:49.100 --> 00:47:52.530
We also have a cluster
group of IPs as well.

00:47:52.530 --> 00:47:54.716
The client facing IPs,
we'd usually-- it's

00:47:54.716 --> 00:47:56.590
up to the customer to
roll into a round robin

00:47:56.590 --> 00:47:59.960
DNS name to have the
clients mount it.

00:47:59.960 --> 00:48:04.750
And they would be pseudo
balancing once at a time.

00:48:04.750 --> 00:48:05.870
This is the core filer.

00:48:05.870 --> 00:48:08.820
Again, this is my Linus machine.

00:48:08.820 --> 00:48:13.810
I have this Linux machine set
in read only mode or re-caching

00:48:13.810 --> 00:48:15.690
where rights are pass through.

00:48:15.690 --> 00:48:19.480
I have a verification with
the core filer at 30 seconds.

00:48:19.480 --> 00:48:21.980
So what this is--
the Avere caching

00:48:21.980 --> 00:48:25.437
layers is essentially
a client to a NAS box.

00:48:25.437 --> 00:48:27.020
And then, in turn,
we're also servers.

00:48:27.020 --> 00:48:29.100
So this verification
of 30 seconds

00:48:29.100 --> 00:48:32.040
is the same as what a client
NFS attribute time will be,

00:48:32.040 --> 00:48:35.310
which would be 30 seconds by
default. I can change that.

00:48:35.310 --> 00:48:36.295
The reason I have this.

00:48:36.295 --> 00:48:38.780
This is my default
set up in case

00:48:38.780 --> 00:48:41.420
you have clients that could
be accessing the storage

00:48:41.420 --> 00:48:44.080
device directly instead
of going through us.

00:48:44.080 --> 00:48:47.667
So Avere caching layer needs
to go back and stat the file

00:48:47.667 --> 00:48:50.000
system for everything that
we have in cache to make sure

00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:52.040
that we have the latest.

00:48:52.040 --> 00:48:54.430
OK.

00:48:54.430 --> 00:48:55.690
Again, I only have one node.

00:48:55.690 --> 00:48:58.130
I could add a node, but
this give some information,

00:48:58.130 --> 00:48:59.240
some good information.

00:48:59.240 --> 00:49:02.650
It shows the IPs that
are client facing.

00:49:02.650 --> 00:49:06.400
For my global namespace,
it gives some performance

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:09.720
specs such as the ops per
second and the hit rate.

00:49:09.720 --> 00:49:12.940
Of course, the hit rate is--
I don't know why it's 50%.

00:49:12.940 --> 00:49:14.330
I'm not sure about that.

00:49:14.330 --> 00:49:17.910
There's no activity going
on, but nonetheless.

00:49:17.910 --> 00:49:19.780
And then the performance.

00:49:19.780 --> 00:49:24.250
We also have a tab here for
clients, hot files, and cache.

00:49:24.250 --> 00:49:26.250
We're not going to see
much here, because I only

00:49:26.250 --> 00:49:26.925
have one client.

00:49:26.925 --> 00:49:28.550
And this client is
actually my MacBook,

00:49:28.550 --> 00:49:31.140
which is through a NAT
device so it's not really

00:49:31.140 --> 00:49:32.710
picking up true IP for it.

00:49:32.710 --> 00:49:34.500
But we would see
a list of IPs that

00:49:34.500 --> 00:49:37.990
would be your clients if
this was something live.

00:49:37.990 --> 00:49:40.441
And then the hot
files is interesting.

00:49:40.441 --> 00:49:42.440
That will give you a whole
list of the hot files

00:49:42.440 --> 00:49:47.270
at any particular point in time
and give you the full path.

00:49:47.270 --> 00:49:48.750
So this is the
full path to a file

00:49:48.750 --> 00:49:50.990
that I was actually accessing
not too long ago, which

00:49:50.990 --> 00:49:53.899
is when I was playing AC/DC.

00:49:53.899 --> 00:49:55.690
Just to wake up this
morning, because there

00:49:55.690 --> 00:49:58.940
was no coffee left, so.

00:49:58.940 --> 00:50:04.170
This is as a new tab that
we just put into the UI.

00:50:04.170 --> 00:50:06.460
And it tells the amount
of space that we're

00:50:06.460 --> 00:50:11.120
using four reads, writes,
total use space, free space,

00:50:11.120 --> 00:50:11.840
et cetera.

00:50:11.840 --> 00:50:13.720
And this is kind of
interesting, especially

00:50:13.720 --> 00:50:16.380
when you have a couple different
core filers sitting behind us.

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:20.710
Every core filer that
we proxy, we actually

00:50:20.710 --> 00:50:23.860
attribute a certain level
of cache in their DRAM,

00:50:23.860 --> 00:50:26.580
as well as the next tier which
would be SSD or SaaS drives,

00:50:26.580 --> 00:50:28.510
depending on the physical box.

00:50:28.510 --> 00:50:30.590
If it's a virtual
instance, then it's

00:50:30.590 --> 00:50:35.260
mostly going to be SSD, the
local SSD or the durable.

00:50:39.280 --> 00:50:41.010
So that's the most exciting tab.

00:50:41.010 --> 00:50:43.287
I'm going to go through
some more boring things here

00:50:43.287 --> 00:50:44.120
