00:51:10.480 --> 00:51:12.350
You see ops per second,
which we had before.

00:51:12.350 --> 00:51:17.410
But this is just a little
bit easier to view.

00:51:17.410 --> 00:51:20.630
And what we can do with this,
we can create any type of graph

00:51:20.630 --> 00:51:21.850
we want.

00:51:21.850 --> 00:51:25.620
So we can add a
graph to the set,

00:51:25.620 --> 00:51:28.240
or we can actually
create a new set.

00:51:28.240 --> 00:51:35.348
But you can actually--
so I'm going

00:51:35.348 --> 00:51:36.806
to call those client
stats, and I'm

00:51:36.806 --> 00:51:38.180
going to choose the
data that I want.

00:51:38.180 --> 00:51:39.680
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:51:39.680 --> 00:51:41.450
to scroll down
and pick something

00:51:41.450 --> 00:51:44.760
that's interesting to us.

00:51:44.760 --> 00:51:48.830
Maybe we'll do ops in
the client network.

00:51:48.830 --> 00:51:52.920
And then what I can do
is I can break this out.

00:51:52.920 --> 00:51:56.860
Say I'm mostly interested in
maybe access calls, lookup

00:51:56.860 --> 00:52:02.600
calls, and get adders,
and read your pluses.

00:52:02.600 --> 00:52:03.745
And then I can create this.

00:52:07.306 --> 00:52:09.430
I've used this actually a
number of different times

00:52:09.430 --> 00:52:13.290
in troubleshooting
some problems.

00:52:13.290 --> 00:52:15.880
So it can be really
flexible and really,

00:52:15.880 --> 00:52:19.820
really important to actually
create these and create

00:52:19.820 --> 00:52:21.430
different graph sets.

00:52:21.430 --> 00:52:23.607
And just pop in there
and take a look at it.

00:52:23.607 --> 00:52:25.940
So you think there's some
kind of high latency going on,

00:52:25.940 --> 00:52:28.020
is your core filer, for
some particular reason,

00:52:28.020 --> 00:52:29.990
and we can kind of
drill down into this.

00:52:35.280 --> 00:52:37.924
So this is the Settings tab.

00:52:37.924 --> 00:52:39.590
I'm not sure if Aaron--
Aaron might have

00:52:39.590 --> 00:52:40.798
touched on this a little bit.

00:52:40.798 --> 00:52:42.500
There's a lot here.

00:52:42.500 --> 00:52:45.940
There's probably no reason to
go through everything here.

00:52:45.940 --> 00:52:50.504
But nonetheless, we'll go
over some of the basic things

00:52:50.504 --> 00:52:51.170
that we've done.

00:52:51.170 --> 00:52:52.870
So there's sections here.

00:52:52.870 --> 00:52:56.680
There's a Vserver which
again, this is the Avere

00:52:56.680 --> 00:52:57.710
GNS that I created.

00:52:57.710 --> 00:53:00.390
This is a representation of all
those other NAS devices sitting

00:53:00.390 --> 00:53:02.600
behind us, or optic
storage devices.

00:53:02.600 --> 00:53:05.370
Then we have the core filer
section, which is again,

00:53:05.370 --> 00:53:07.080
you're basically
defining the core filer

00:53:07.080 --> 00:53:11.650
prior to creating a
