01:07:03.650 --> 01:07:05.650
So over the last
several years, we've

01:07:05.650 --> 01:07:09.820
really seen a shift from the
public perception of Cloud

01:07:09.820 --> 01:07:14.770
not necessarily being as secure
as your on-premise environment

01:07:14.770 --> 01:07:17.960
to a flip-flop where
Cloud is really

01:07:17.960 --> 01:07:20.290
the standard for security.

01:07:20.290 --> 01:07:22.870
When you look at the major
public cloud providers,

01:07:22.870 --> 01:07:24.920
and Google being one
of them, of course,

01:07:24.920 --> 01:07:27.500
it is our business
to protect user data.

01:07:27.500 --> 01:07:33.010
We have billions of users
both on the enterprise

01:07:33.010 --> 01:07:34.180
and on the individual level.

01:07:34.180 --> 01:07:35.940
And maintaining the
integrity of that data

01:07:35.940 --> 01:07:38.440
is really critical and
core to our business.

01:07:38.440 --> 01:07:41.180
So as you can see here,
these are typically

01:07:41.180 --> 01:07:43.440
a lot of the types of
breaches that happen

01:07:43.440 --> 01:07:44.830
in on-premise environments.

01:07:44.830 --> 01:07:47.160
And we've seen some pretty
high profile breaches.

01:07:47.160 --> 01:07:49.690
You can see up there
Sony, Target, Home Depot,

01:07:49.690 --> 01:07:52.280
and Ashley Madison
happen in which user data

01:07:52.280 --> 01:07:55.080
has been exposed to the public.

01:07:55.080 --> 01:07:56.960
So what we have at
Google is really

01:07:56.960 --> 01:07:59.240
the end to end supply chain.

01:07:59.240 --> 01:08:01.680
We build our own servers.

01:08:01.680 --> 01:08:04.000
We write the
hypervisor software.

01:08:04.000 --> 01:08:06.790
We lay the groundwork
for the fibre.

01:08:06.790 --> 01:08:10.490
This is all integrated
in a single offering

01:08:10.490 --> 01:08:14.060
that Google Cloud Platform now
makes publicly available to you

01:08:14.060 --> 01:08:14.900
as the user.

01:08:14.900 --> 01:08:18.340
And keep in mind from
a holistic overview,

01:08:18.340 --> 01:08:20.729
Google Cloud Platform is
the same infrastructure

01:08:20.729 --> 01:08:21.850
that powers Google.

01:08:21.850 --> 01:08:23.680
So when I talk about
the need for Google

01:08:23.680 --> 01:08:27.640
as a corporation to
protect user data,

01:08:27.640 --> 01:08:30.410
it's not just on the
enterprise level where

01:08:30.410 --> 01:08:32.670
you might be using it for
rendering or for transcoding

01:08:32.670 --> 01:08:33.500
or for storage.

01:08:33.500 --> 01:08:36.819
But it is the very
fabric of what keeps

01:08:36.819 --> 01:08:40.729
Google running as a company.

01:08:40.729 --> 01:08:43.729
And in terms of
the different steps

01:08:43.729 --> 01:08:48.500
we take to build this end to
end solution, secure solution,

01:08:48.500 --> 01:08:50.740
it really starts
with our people.

01:08:50.740 --> 01:08:54.279
We have over 500 security
professionals, many of whom

01:08:54.279 --> 01:08:57.870
literally have like written
the book on security.

01:08:57.870 --> 01:08:59.729
And these people are
employed by Google.

01:08:59.729 --> 01:09:01.189
We are the first
company to start

01:09:01.189 --> 01:09:05.189
offering a Reward Vulnerability
Program to incentivize people

01:09:05.189 --> 01:09:08.319
to try to break us, to
try to find loopholes

01:09:08.319 --> 01:09:11.250
and addressing those
and fixing those.

01:09:11.250 --> 01:09:14.090
And if you think about
Google, again, as a platform,

01:09:14.090 --> 01:09:17.149
we are among the most targeted
platforms in the world.

01:09:17.149 --> 01:09:21.140
And that goes everywhere from
nation-state-sponsored attacks

01:09:21.140 --> 01:09:23.010
all the way down to
the individual hacker

01:09:23.010 --> 01:09:25.010
sitting in their basement
trying to get through.

01:09:25.010 --> 01:09:28.490
So critical to our
operation as a business

01:09:28.490 --> 01:09:30.680
is the security aspect of it.

01:09:30.680 --> 01:09:33.479
So I've spoken a little bit
about the people aspect of it.

01:09:33.479 --> 01:09:38.255
From a literal, physical, data
center security standpoint,

01:09:38.255 --> 01:09:40.130
there's actually a video
that's up on YouTube

01:09:40.130 --> 01:09:44.279
showing the advanced access
restrictions we have.

01:09:44.279 --> 01:09:46.050
It's fully biometric.

01:09:46.050 --> 01:09:49.720
Myself, as a Google employee, I
cannot get into a data center.

01:09:49.720 --> 01:09:51.220
These things are
very locked down,

01:09:51.220 --> 01:09:55.010
very restricted in terms of who
actually has physical access

01:09:55.010 --> 01:09:57.770
to even operate within
these data centers.

01:09:57.770 --> 01:10:00.160
And then, again,
we build the stack.

01:10:00.160 --> 01:10:02.590
We're one of the top five
server manufacturers.

01:10:02.590 --> 01:10:05.590
We build the hardware,
labeled the hypervisor,

01:10:05.590 --> 01:10:07.040
we've laid the network.

01:10:07.040 --> 01:10:11.900
Everything end to end
is controlled by Google.

01:10:11.900 --> 01:10:16.190
So what are we doing on the
broader scale for security?

01:10:16.190 --> 01:10:19.470
Well, these are all the
typical security certifications

01:10:19.470 --> 01:10:25.300
that you would see, stuff like
ISO 2701, SOC 2, SOC 3, FISMA,

01:10:25.300 --> 01:10:26.850
HIPAA, et cetera.

01:10:26.850 --> 01:10:30.480
And you can go to
cloud.google.com/security

01:10:30.480 --> 01:10:34.530
to read up more about the things
we're doing within the broader

01:10:34.530 --> 01:10:35.370
security spectrum.

01:10:35.370 --> 01:10:38.240
But I understand what's really
important for all of you

01:10:38.240 --> 01:10:40.950
in this room is how
are we addressing

01:10:40.950 --> 01:10:44.500
the studio specific
requirements of Cloud

01:10:44.500 --> 01:10:46.460
in today's environment.

01:10:46.460 --> 01:10:48.460
And there are several
things we've done.

01:10:48.460 --> 01:10:53.050
And I'll walk you through
those on a step by step basis.

01:10:53.050 --> 01:10:56.250
I think it's important to note--
and most of you in the room,

01:10:56.250 --> 01:10:59.890
I think, are aware of this, to
understand the MPAA's position

01:10:59.890 --> 01:11:00.550
on Cloud.

01:11:00.550 --> 01:11:05.190
So while they have published
a best practices guideline

01:11:05.190 --> 01:11:08.760
for Cloud, there is
currently no certification.

01:11:08.760 --> 01:11:11.700
There is no MPAA logo program.

01:11:11.700 --> 01:11:15.830
Undergoing an audit for Cloud
with the MPAA does not get you

01:11:15.830 --> 01:11:17.770
clearance with the studios.

01:11:17.770 --> 01:11:20.540
So we've spoken to
the MPAA about this.

01:11:20.540 --> 01:11:24.350
But what we've decided to do
is work directly and closely

01:11:24.350 --> 01:11:28.490
with the studios, directly to
educate them about Google Cloud

01:11:28.490 --> 01:11:32.290
Platform, inform them of
our security and compliance,

01:11:32.290 --> 01:11:35.140
and in some cases,
actually undergo

01:11:35.140 --> 01:11:39.150
an independent third party audit
at the request of the studios

01:11:39.150 --> 01:11:43.300
to certify Google Cloud
Platform for Tier-1

01:11:43.300 --> 01:11:45.877
pre-release theatrical content.

01:11:45.877 --> 01:11:47.460
So I think the
important thing to note

01:11:47.460 --> 01:11:51.570
is that we've examined
the issues here.

01:11:51.570 --> 01:11:54.210
We've examined where
is the real value

01:11:54.210 --> 01:11:56.010
in having security audits.

01:11:56.010 --> 01:12:00.750
I think we've started at the
top with the most difficult

01:12:00.750 --> 01:12:03.820
and strict studios
as far security

01:12:03.820 --> 01:12:06.690
goes, and have now taken
that information, taken

01:12:06.690 --> 01:12:11.330
that certification, and are
working with the other studios

01:12:11.330 --> 01:12:13.925
to essentially vet out
Google Cloud Platform.

01:12:13.925 --> 01:12:16.300
And again, this is not going
to be an open the floodgates

01:12:16.300 --> 01:12:17.410
to everyone.

01:12:17.410 --> 01:12:19.300
As this is a very
new technology,

01:12:19.300 --> 01:12:21.780
we know studios are somewhat
hesitant to embrace us.

01:12:21.780 --> 01:12:23.113
And they're worried about leaks.

01:12:23.113 --> 01:12:26.570
We're talking about billion
dollar tent-pole films and data

01:12:26.570 --> 01:12:27.070
getting out.

01:12:27.070 --> 01:12:29.670
It really is now on
a case by case basis.

01:12:29.670 --> 01:12:32.280
But it's important to understand
how closely we are working

01:12:32.280 --> 01:12:35.320
with these studios, how
we're working to provide

01:12:35.320 --> 01:12:37.250
prescriptive hardening
guides to show

01:12:37.250 --> 01:12:39.850
the implementation
of your pipeline

01:12:39.850 --> 01:12:42.300
on Google Cloud
Platform to enable

01:12:42.300 --> 01:12:45.130
pre-release theatrical
rendering happening.

01:12:45.130 --> 01:12:47.190
And as part of the
work we've been doing,

01:12:47.190 --> 01:12:49.250
there's some things
we'll be publishing

01:12:49.250 --> 01:12:51.900
by the end of this
quarter on our website.

01:12:51.900 --> 01:12:54.680
And all of this material is
now available directly to you.

01:12:54.680 --> 01:12:57.200
You can contact Lauren, and
she has access to all of this

01:12:57.200 --> 01:12:59.690
and can provide this
to you ahead of time.

01:12:59.690 --> 01:13:04.110
But we will be providing
this independent audit

01:13:04.110 --> 01:13:08.380
that we've undergone showing how
we map to the MPAA standards.

01:13:08.380 --> 01:13:11.820
So again, while not necessarily
giving us the ability

01:13:11.820 --> 01:13:15.150
to get a Cloud Certification
through the MPAA,

01:13:15.150 --> 01:13:17.046
this is at least
showing how we do map

01:13:17.046 --> 01:13:18.420
to those standards
for any of you

01:13:18.420 --> 01:13:21.070
that are concerned about that.

01:13:21.070 --> 01:13:25.080
And we'll also be publishing a
best practices hardening guide

01:13:25.080 --> 01:13:29.440
for how to safely and securely
deploy your cloud rendering

01:13:29.440 --> 01:13:32.010
solution on Google
Cloud Platform.

01:13:32.010 --> 01:13:33.820
So in the end,
this is really all

01:13:33.820 --> 01:13:36.410
about giving you, the
customers, control

01:13:36.410 --> 01:13:39.880
over your security while at the
same time understanding that

01:13:39.880 --> 01:13:42.130
within this industry
specifically,

01:13:42.130 --> 01:13:46.170
there are definite requirements
and needs and relationships

01:13:46.170 --> 01:13:50.570
that need to be maintained
with the Big Six studios who

01:13:50.570 --> 01:13:52.660
are giving you much
of your work when it

01:13:52.660 --> 01:13:54.490
comes to feature film content.

01:13:54.490 --> 01:13:58.420
And I'm confident to say that
we've done a great job so far.

01:13:58.420 --> 01:14:00.370
And I'm really looking
forward to a lot

01:14:00.370 --> 01:14:02.078
of the things we'll
be able to talk about

01:14:02.078 --> 01:14:04.710
as the year progresses
in terms of projects

01:14:04.710 --> 01:14:09.120
done on GCP for major
feature film releases.

01:14:09.120 --> 01:14:13.330
So next, I am going to turn
it over to our panelists.

01:14:13.330 --> 01:14:14.830
I believe Jeff
Kember from Google

01:14:14.830 --> 01:14:17.190
will be up, along with
Jim Thompson from Avere.

01:14:17.190 --> 01:14:19.879
And I'd also like to introduce
the folks from Eight VFX who

01:14:19.879 --> 01:14:20.670
are going to speak.

01:14:20.670 --> 01:14:22.540
So we'll go ahead and
get the chairs set up

01:14:22.540 --> 01:14:24.180
and start our panel
in just a moment.

01:14:34.262 --> 01:14:35.220
JEFF KEMBER: Hey, guys.

01:14:35.220 --> 01:14:38.400
And just while we're
getting the chairs set up,

01:14:38.400 --> 01:14:40.330
there's just one thing
I wanted to point out.

01:14:40.330 --> 01:14:43.130
So Google does not
have an official GPU

01:14:43.130 --> 01:14:44.660
offering at this time.

01:14:44.660 --> 01:14:48.810
But what we do have is an
interest in hearing from you on

01:14:48.810 --> 01:14:49.980
do you want them?

01:14:49.980 --> 01:14:51.850
If you do, come see me after.

01:14:51.850 --> 01:14:55.910
And let me know if you want
compute GPUs, or display GPUs,

01:14:55.910 --> 01:14:57.989
how many you might want,
how you might use them.

01:14:57.989 --> 01:14:59.780
We're just kind of
taking some information.

01:14:59.780 --> 01:15:02.030
So we have a little survey
that we can send you,

01:15:02.030 --> 01:15:04.680
as well, that you can fill
out a little more concrete.

01:15:04.680 --> 01:15:07.430
So again, as we all know,
most of the compute loads

01:15:07.430 --> 01:15:11.610
are render on CPU, but there
are remote desktop workloads

01:15:11.610 --> 01:15:14.212
and other magic things
that happen well on a GPU.

01:15:14.212 --> 01:15:16.170
So please come and talk
with me after the show.

01:15:16.170 --> 01:15:17.950
Or actually, can I
see a show of hands?

01:15:17.950 --> 01:15:20.530
Who's interested in GPUs?

01:15:20.530 --> 01:15:21.570
OK, that's pretty good.

01:15:21.570 --> 01:15:22.070
I like that.

01:15:22.070 --> 01:15:23.140
In fact, I want
to take a picture

01:15:23.140 --> 01:15:25.598
of that for the Product Manager
because he asked me to ask.

01:15:25.598 --> 01:15:27.580
So hands up again, please.

01:15:27.580 --> 01:15:29.700
Awesome, OK, and got it.

01:15:29.700 --> 01:15:32.460
Thank you very much.

01:15:32.460 --> 01:15:35.680
TODD PRIVES: OK, so we now
have our panel discussion.

01:15:35.680 --> 01:15:37.970
I'd like to bring
Jeff Kember back

01:15:37.970 --> 01:15:42.270
on stage, Jim Thompson, and
Damien, and Philippe from Eight

01:15:42.270 --> 01:15:43.400
VFX.

01:15:43.400 --> 01:15:46.060
Who have actually gone ahead and
deployed a rendering pipeline

01:15:46.060 --> 01:15:48.200
on GCP.

01:15:48.200 --> 01:15:49.990
Does everyone have microphones?

01:15:49.990 --> 01:15:53.390
Or am I going to be
passing the microphone?

01:15:53.390 --> 01:15:54.117
OK.

01:15:54.117 --> 01:15:55.450
So Damien, do you want to start?

01:15:55.450 --> 01:15:57.491
Just give a little bit of
an overview about Eight

01:15:57.491 --> 01:15:59.020
VFX, some of the
work you've done,

01:15:59.020 --> 01:16:01.305
and your decision to move
to Google Cloud Platform,

01:16:01.305 --> 01:16:02.430
and how you've set that up?

01:16:04.809 --> 01:16:06.350
DAMIEN BATAILLE: So
as you know, when

01:16:06.350 --> 01:16:10.530
you are a company that with some
visual effect for commercial,

01:16:10.530 --> 01:16:16.540
you have something to
render quickly, some image.

01:16:16.540 --> 01:16:19.410
And because we have
a local render farm,

01:16:19.410 --> 01:16:20.610
but we have to extend.

01:16:20.610 --> 01:16:25.940
We have to have
more power to render

01:16:25.940 --> 01:16:27.820
this image which
is why we choose

01:16:27.820 --> 01:16:30.990
to render on the Google Cloud.

01:16:30.990 --> 01:16:35.650
And what else?

01:16:35.650 --> 01:16:37.290
TODD PRIVES: Great
start, absolutely.

01:16:37.290 --> 01:16:41.060
Philippe, do you have
anything else to add to that?

01:16:41.060 --> 01:16:45.430
PHILIPPE CHOTARD: Yes, so
excuse my English to begin with.

01:16:45.430 --> 01:16:49.760
So we are a a very
small graphics studio.

01:16:49.760 --> 01:16:53.330
We have about 20 artists.

01:16:53.330 --> 01:16:56.470
So I know there is a list
with very big companies

01:16:56.470 --> 01:16:58.170
here on the right.

01:16:58.170 --> 01:17:02.820
And it looks like
Avere and Google Cloud

01:17:02.820 --> 01:17:07.880
is made for big
companies, but it's not.

01:17:07.880 --> 01:17:11.060
We have very specific needs.

01:17:11.060 --> 01:17:12.800
When we need to
do some rendering,

01:17:12.800 --> 01:17:14.990
we don't need
thousands of calls.

01:17:14.990 --> 01:17:21.330
But we have local small
on-premise render farm.

01:17:21.330 --> 01:17:27.340
So we sometime need to fire
up some up or on the Cloud.

01:17:27.340 --> 01:17:32.590
So it's started for every
kind of company, the big one

01:17:32.590 --> 01:17:35.571
and the small one, like us.

01:17:35.571 --> 01:17:37.320
TODD PRIVES: Great,
so we have a few panel

01:17:37.320 --> 01:17:39.653
questions I'd like to start
with and then certainly take

01:17:39.653 --> 01:17:42.011
some questions from
the audience as well.

01:17:42.011 --> 01:17:44.010
I think one of the things
I'd like to start with

01:17:44.010 --> 01:17:46.150
is talking about latency.

01:17:46.150 --> 01:17:47.840
Good question with
the Avere folks

01:17:47.840 --> 01:17:49.320
and with the Eight VFX folks.

01:17:49.320 --> 01:17:51.460
So how does Cloud impact
performance for you?

01:17:51.460 --> 01:17:53.140
And how does that
affect your pipeline?

01:17:53.140 --> 01:17:57.880
And how have you managed to
essentially push assets up

01:17:57.880 --> 01:18:00.730
for optimal rendering?

01:18:00.730 --> 01:18:05.110
DAMIEN BATAILLE: I know that
normally we can't-- for me,

01:18:05.110 --> 01:18:08.360
the problem was that because we
are using a lot of custom data

01:18:08.360 --> 01:18:12.940
inside a scene, like a lot
of texture and geometry,

01:18:12.940 --> 01:18:16.180
the first solution that we try
is just to use some [INAUDIBLE]

01:18:16.180 --> 01:18:20.880
up putting to scan all the scene
and from what we have to send

01:18:20.880 --> 01:18:23.410
to the Cloud before it render.

01:18:23.410 --> 01:18:26.450
And for me, it was
not a good solution

01:18:26.450 --> 01:18:31.000
which is why I talk about
this with Philippe here

01:18:31.000 --> 01:18:35.250
and I ask him if it is
possible to have something that

01:18:35.250 --> 01:18:40.130
can transfer over in real time
between my company and Google

01:18:40.130 --> 01:18:41.440
Cloud.

01:18:41.440 --> 01:18:45.140
And Philippe told
me that there's

01:18:45.140 --> 01:18:48.070
a solution which is Avere.

01:18:48.070 --> 01:18:52.370
And I think he can
talk about this.

01:18:52.370 --> 01:18:55.440
PHILIPPE CHOTARD: So
in my previous company,

01:18:55.440 --> 01:19:00.405
we had physical Avere cluster.

01:19:00.405 --> 01:19:04.360
It was composed of six nodes.

01:19:04.360 --> 01:19:10.800
And we add about five
petabytes worth of data.

01:19:10.800 --> 01:19:13.270
So this was how look at storage.

01:19:13.270 --> 01:19:22.140
And before having Avere, we
really had a lot of IO issue.

01:19:22.140 --> 01:19:25.210
And working with
Avere really help us.

01:19:25.210 --> 01:19:32.950
So in this company, I wanted
to give a try to the cloud

01:19:32.950 --> 01:19:38.950
solution because when we began
to do the testing of rendering

01:19:38.950 --> 01:19:43.680
on the Cloud, we were
directly connecting our render

01:19:43.680 --> 01:19:48.090
farm to our local
on-premise storage.

01:19:48.090 --> 01:19:49.670
And it was awful.

01:19:49.670 --> 01:19:53.640
So we began with Windows.

01:19:53.640 --> 01:19:56.800
We are full Windows studio.

01:19:56.800 --> 01:19:59.355
So our clients are
Windows machine.

01:19:59.355 --> 01:20:02.270
Our storage is a
Windows machine.

01:20:02.270 --> 01:20:04.360
So yeah, basically
it's full Windows.

01:20:04.360 --> 01:20:07.660
So when we began to do the
rendering on the Cloud,

01:20:07.660 --> 01:20:11.900
we had some huge latency
issue that were not

01:20:11.900 --> 01:20:17.530
only due to the number of
access done by the render,

01:20:17.530 --> 01:20:23.990
but also because when
you use SMB from a one,

01:20:23.990 --> 01:20:29.620
from a VPN connection, you will
have a very, very low transfer.

01:20:29.620 --> 01:20:32.140
Because SMB is not
a protocol that

01:20:32.140 --> 01:20:35.550
has been thought to
be used over one,

01:20:35.550 --> 01:20:39.360
because it's a very
chatty protocol.

01:20:39.360 --> 01:20:43.830
So we had to use NFS
between the cloud

01:20:43.830 --> 01:20:47.100
and the on-premise storage.

01:20:47.100 --> 01:20:50.690
So Avere was a good
solution for that,

01:20:50.690 --> 01:20:53.300
because we would
put future nodes

01:20:53.300 --> 01:20:58.350
on the cloud that would connect
to our on-premise storage

01:20:58.350 --> 01:21:00.090
directly.

01:21:00.090 --> 01:21:03.990
And using NFS, so we
wouldn't have any kind

01:21:03.990 --> 01:21:05.890
of those latency issue.

01:21:05.890 --> 01:21:09.400
And it will, of course,
help us because we

01:21:09.400 --> 01:21:14.590
had a lot of virtual
render on the cloud that

01:21:14.590 --> 01:21:18.410
will read the same files.

01:21:18.410 --> 01:21:23.090
So basically, when you
were using the [INAUDIBLE]

01:21:23.090 --> 01:21:26.230
caching system, you
read one file at once.

01:21:26.230 --> 01:21:32.540
If you have like 100
nodes on the cloud,

01:21:32.540 --> 01:21:36.090
and they access the same file
that we already redefined once.

01:21:36.090 --> 01:21:39.290
So yeah, it was a huge benefit.

01:21:39.290 --> 01:21:42.210
TODD PRIVES: So along those
lines, Jim, Avere as a filer.

01:21:42.210 --> 01:21:44.220
When would you choose
or why should I choose

01:21:44.220 --> 01:21:45.770
Avere over other filer options?

01:21:45.770 --> 01:21:48.455
What's the ideal workflow
scenario for that?

01:21:48.455 --> 01:21:50.330
JIM THOMPSON: Yeah,
anything that's cachable.

01:21:50.330 --> 01:21:51.800
Again, Avere is a caching layer.

01:21:51.800 --> 01:21:54.857
So if there's
something that-- and it

01:21:54.857 --> 01:21:56.690
won't work for everything,
absolutely right.

01:21:56.690 --> 01:21:59.990
You really have to
understand your flow and what

01:21:59.990 --> 01:22:02.290
you're doing as far as
a render is concerned.

01:22:02.290 --> 01:22:04.994
If there's going to be
repeatedly access content

01:22:04.994 --> 01:22:06.660
and assets that you
need for the render,

01:22:06.660 --> 01:22:08.760
the Avere layer,
caching layer is

01:22:08.760 --> 01:22:12.770
going to be very important
to cache all that stuff.

01:22:12.770 --> 01:22:14.780
Because otherwise, each
individual render node

01:22:14.780 --> 01:22:19.190
is going to access the
storage, your on-prem storage,

01:22:19.190 --> 01:22:20.640
over and over and over again.

01:22:20.640 --> 01:22:23.179
So it's going to consume
all that throughput and all

01:22:23.179 --> 01:22:24.970
that bandwidth that
you have from the cloud

01:22:24.970 --> 01:22:26.440
back on to on-prem.

01:22:26.440 --> 01:22:29.350
So having us in the picture
there, in the data stream,

01:22:29.350 --> 01:22:32.440
is going to alleviate
all that latency.

01:22:32.440 --> 01:22:35.480
I mean, not all of
it but most of it

01:22:35.480 --> 01:22:37.430
from accessing all
that data that you're

01:22:37.430 --> 01:22:39.370
on-prem and eventually
get your renders done

01:22:39.370 --> 01:22:43.037
in a timely manner instead
of taking a much longer time.

01:22:43.037 --> 01:22:43.870
TODD PRIVES: Thanks.

01:22:43.870 --> 01:22:44.900
Jeff, next question for you.

01:22:44.900 --> 01:22:47.316
Having worked with studios all
over the world now in terms

01:22:47.316 --> 01:22:50.430
of getting them set up with
VFX rendering pipelines,

01:22:50.430 --> 01:22:53.550
what do you see as these studios
finding the single biggest

01:22:53.550 --> 01:22:56.370
challenge to getting
on to Cloud being?

01:22:56.370 --> 01:22:58.420
JEFF KEMBER: I would
say the single biggest

01:22:58.420 --> 01:23:02.370
challenge is the social
engineering with production.

01:23:02.370 --> 01:23:04.380
The idea that well,
the artists are just

01:23:04.380 --> 01:23:06.660
going to use all the disk
space and all the compute,

01:23:06.660 --> 01:23:08.390
and pricing is going
to be a problem.

01:23:08.390 --> 01:23:10.190
And how do we manage this.

01:23:10.190 --> 01:23:12.660
I touched on it a
little bit earlier,

01:23:12.660 --> 01:23:16.830
the idea of producing a
matrix, a spreadsheet of all

01:23:16.830 --> 01:23:18.720
the various performance
aspects and also

01:23:18.720 --> 01:23:21.370
trying to come up with a cost
for what render looks like.

01:23:21.370 --> 01:23:24.780
But it really comes
down to, in my mind,

01:23:24.780 --> 01:23:26.570
talking with
production and having

01:23:26.570 --> 01:23:29.280
them manage how many machines
get spun up and such.

01:23:29.280 --> 01:23:30.710
Or you add [INAUDIBLE] to it.

01:23:30.710 --> 01:23:32.840
But no, there are--
the questions came up.

01:23:32.840 --> 01:23:34.550
I was in a meeting yesterday
where it was like, well,

01:23:34.550 --> 01:23:35.920
do we want the render
wranglers to do it?

01:23:35.920 --> 01:23:37.780
Or do we want systems and
pipeline to manage it?

01:23:37.780 --> 01:23:40.071
Well, no, it's always going
to be a production problem.

01:23:40.071 --> 01:23:42.002
And that's kind of the
human factors issue.

01:23:42.002 --> 01:23:43.460
The excellent work
that's been done

01:23:43.460 --> 01:23:48.360
on security with Todd and
Matto and others, that's

01:23:48.360 --> 01:23:49.860
like a solved problem
at this point.

01:23:49.860 --> 01:23:51.234
And it's just sort
of propagating

01:23:51.234 --> 01:23:52.826
throughout the industry.

01:23:52.826 --> 01:23:54.700
There's obviously
additional work to be done,

01:23:54.700 --> 01:23:56.420
but that isn't the
elephant in the room

01:23:56.420 --> 01:23:58.170
to talk about when
we have file system

01:23:58.170 --> 01:24:00.520
support and direct
connect and licensing

01:24:00.520 --> 01:24:03.330
is a problem that is going away.

01:24:03.330 --> 01:24:05.370
So really, it
just, for my money,

01:24:05.370 --> 01:24:08.080
it comes down to
adoption in the workplace

01:24:08.080 --> 01:24:11.080
and how to best engineer that.

01:24:11.080 --> 01:24:12.580
TODD PRIVES: Back
to the Eight guys,

01:24:12.580 --> 01:24:14.980
Jeff mentioned cost and
total cost of ownership.

01:24:14.980 --> 01:24:18.680
So obviously you have a small
on-premise render farm now.

01:24:18.680 --> 01:24:21.110
At what point do you decide
to put jobs on the Cloud?

01:24:21.110 --> 01:24:23.420
And what sort of cost
analysis or comparisons

01:24:23.420 --> 01:24:27.189
have you done to what it
costs to run a render locally

01:24:27.189 --> 01:24:28.480
versus putting it on the cloud?

01:24:28.480 --> 01:24:30.350
And what additional,
I guess benefits,

01:24:30.350 --> 01:24:32.920
beyond just the
one to one dollars

01:24:32.920 --> 01:24:36.020
do you see using
rendering on Cloud?

01:24:36.020 --> 01:24:41.650
PHILIPPE CHOTARD: So on my
point of view as an IT guy,

01:24:41.650 --> 01:24:47.160
I didn't really think about
it as a cost point of view.

01:24:47.160 --> 01:24:51.740
But just the fact that when
you need to do something fast,

01:24:51.740 --> 01:24:56.650
you have to have the
[INAUDIBLE] right now.

01:24:56.650 --> 01:25:00.140
So if you want to see
it on the cost view,

01:25:00.140 --> 01:25:02.730
usually when we
need to render stuff

01:25:02.730 --> 01:25:05.210
that we need to
render fast, and we

01:25:05.210 --> 01:25:09.890
don't have enough power on
site, we just rent machines.

01:25:09.890 --> 01:25:12.710
Renting machine usually
you rent a machine

01:25:12.710 --> 01:25:15.430
for one week, one month.

01:25:15.430 --> 01:25:21.590
Using the Cloud, we just fire
up how many machine we want.

01:25:21.590 --> 01:25:24.430
If we want five render,
we launch five render.

01:25:24.430 --> 01:25:27.830
If we want 100 render, we
just we just launch them.

01:25:27.830 --> 01:25:32.560
And it's for the
time we need them.

01:25:32.560 --> 01:25:38.700
So if they render will take 12
hour, we will pay for 12 hour,

01:25:38.700 --> 01:25:41.700
not for one week.

01:25:41.700 --> 01:25:44.090
TODD PRIVES: And then
in terms of costing out

01:25:44.090 --> 01:25:47.875
what it costs on Cloud, are you
able to bill these jobs back

01:25:47.875 --> 01:25:48.500
to the clients?

01:25:48.500 --> 01:25:51.100
Or do you figure it into the
actual cost of production?

01:25:51.100 --> 01:25:56.116
Or how does that side of it fit
into your production schedules?

01:25:56.116 --> 01:25:57.990
DAMIEN BATAILLE: Before
to start the project,

01:25:57.990 --> 01:26:04.580
we try to know what will be the
costs of the rendering side.

01:26:04.580 --> 01:26:12.150
And we try to add a maximum
of three render on the Cloud

01:26:12.150 --> 01:26:14.540
which is why, before we
have a project, and we know,

01:26:14.540 --> 01:26:18.670
so this is [INAUDIBLE]
inside the production.

01:26:18.670 --> 01:26:20.670
TODD PRIVES: Jim, back
to you on the Avere side.

01:26:20.670 --> 01:26:22.510
So we're starting to
see more and more small

01:26:22.510 --> 01:26:23.968
studios that don't
necessarily have

01:26:23.968 --> 01:26:26.879
an Avere on-premise
caching appliance deploying

01:26:26.879 --> 01:26:27.670
Avere on the cloud.

01:26:27.670 --> 01:26:31.317
Moonbot Studios in Louisiana
is a great example of that.

01:26:31.317 --> 01:26:33.150
What information can
you give us about that?

01:26:33.150 --> 01:26:34.900
And share the
advantages to studios

01:26:34.900 --> 01:26:36.525
that might not
necessarily be investing

01:26:36.525 --> 01:26:38.275
in an on-prem appliance,
but still getting

01:26:38.275 --> 01:26:40.590
the benefits of being able
to set up a scalable Cloud

01:26:40.590 --> 01:26:42.410
filer with cache.

01:26:42.410 --> 01:26:44.980
JIM THOMPSON: Yeah, similar
to what Phillipe mentioned

01:26:44.980 --> 01:26:48.147
here is that any time you need
some type of burst computing,

01:26:48.147 --> 01:26:49.480
and this is what Moonbot needed.

01:26:49.480 --> 01:26:52.089
They needed to get
some rendering done

01:26:52.089 --> 01:26:52.880
in a timely manner.

01:26:52.880 --> 01:26:57.490
In order to do that, it was just
physically impossible to get

01:26:57.490 --> 01:26:59.867
stuff on-prem to do that.

01:26:59.867 --> 01:27:01.450
They could afford
it, but the thing is

01:27:01.450 --> 01:27:03.241
is that they just didn't
have enough space.

01:27:03.241 --> 01:27:06.000
And there's other power
issues and other things.

01:27:06.000 --> 01:27:08.920
And this is for it could
be anybody, absolutely,

01:27:08.920 --> 01:27:13.370
across any type of environment.

01:27:13.370 --> 01:27:16.137
So what they did is they had
to think about different ways

01:27:16.137 --> 01:27:16.720
of doing this.

01:27:16.720 --> 01:27:19.053
The only way to do that was
to actually put something up

01:27:19.053 --> 01:27:23.510
in the Cloud and utilize the
cores to get the jobs done.

01:27:23.510 --> 01:27:25.420
And the thing is, like
Philippe mentioned

01:27:25.420 --> 01:27:27.760
as well, is that you
could spin up 100 cores,

01:27:27.760 --> 01:27:30.490
you could spend up 2,000,
you could spin up 30,000,

01:27:30.490 --> 01:27:32.610
depends on how quickly
you want the job done.

01:27:32.610 --> 01:27:34.120
And that's what they did.

01:27:34.120 --> 01:27:36.310
They actually spun up--
I can't remember exactly,

01:27:36.310 --> 01:27:37.930
but I think it was
over 5,000 cores

01:27:37.930 --> 01:27:42.080
at a time to render to
get their jobs done.

01:27:42.080 --> 01:27:44.040
It's really that simple.

01:27:44.040 --> 01:27:47.430
If you need something done
in a critical timeline,

01:27:47.430 --> 01:27:49.150
you have to think
outside of the box

01:27:49.150 --> 01:27:50.370
to actually find a solution.

01:27:50.370 --> 01:27:52.260
And this is the
only way to do it.

01:27:52.260 --> 01:27:54.240
TODD PRIVES: Great,
so back to you guys

01:27:54.240 --> 01:27:57.090
on the whole job
management side of things,

01:27:57.090 --> 01:28:00.620
since there is this concern
about costs on the Cloud,

01:28:00.620 --> 01:28:01.930
how do you manage submissions?

01:28:01.930 --> 01:28:04.620
Is it done through a
typical artist workflow?

01:28:04.620 --> 01:28:07.060
Or is centralized to a render
wrangler or a different way

01:28:07.060 --> 01:28:08.010
in your pipeline?

01:28:08.010 --> 01:28:11.610
DAMIEN BATAILLE: Yeah, so
currently on the artist side,

01:28:11.610 --> 01:28:12.801
this is just one check box.

01:28:12.801 --> 01:28:15.300
You have to choose if you want
to render on the local render

01:28:15.300 --> 01:28:18.080
farm or the Cloud render farm.

01:28:18.080 --> 01:28:20.360
So we are using Deadline.

01:28:20.360 --> 01:28:23.740
And the process is
exactly the same

01:28:23.740 --> 01:28:26.370
between the Cloud and
the local render farm.

01:28:26.370 --> 01:28:28.910
So on the pipeline
side, I didn't

01:28:28.910 --> 01:28:33.560
have to do a lot of change.

01:28:33.560 --> 01:28:35.440
So [INAUDIBLE] is
when user choose

01:28:35.440 --> 01:28:40.130
to use a Cloud rendering,
we send the job

01:28:40.130 --> 01:28:43.370
into the [INAUDIBLE] and
then we generate some--

01:28:43.370 --> 01:28:45.990
we are using Avere,
so the render farm

01:28:45.990 --> 01:28:49.860
will generate from
Maya or the VR scene.

01:28:49.860 --> 01:28:51.590
And we always use the
local render farm.

01:28:51.590 --> 01:28:56.440
And after another job, we take
all the VR scene and render

01:28:56.440 --> 01:29:00.179
all of them inside the-- to the
Cloud from the cloud rendering.

01:29:00.179 --> 01:29:02.220
TODD PRIVES: And Jeff,
what are your observations

01:29:02.220 --> 01:29:03.969
working with the really
large studios that

01:29:03.969 --> 01:29:06.010
are using 30,000 40,000
50,000 cores on GCP?

01:29:06.010 --> 01:29:09.535
How do they manage artist
submission of jobs to Cloud?

01:29:09.535 --> 01:29:10.660
JEFF KEMBER: Good question.

01:29:10.660 --> 01:29:12.560
Yeah, we have some
companies that

01:29:12.560 --> 01:29:15.900
are running more than
60,000 cores on a job.

01:29:15.900 --> 01:29:19.060
You run into a scenario where
you have a ride film come in.

01:29:19.060 --> 01:29:22.930
And it's 6 or 12k or 24k,
because it's a dome projection.

01:29:22.930 --> 01:29:25.180
And you just don't have the
multi-hundred gig machines

01:29:25.180 --> 01:29:27.140
in quantity with 64
cores sitting around.

01:29:27.140 --> 01:29:29.550
So they're expensive.

01:29:29.550 --> 01:29:32.150
But you can get them from us.

01:29:32.150 --> 01:29:33.805
So in terms of
submitting jobs, some

01:29:33.805 --> 01:29:35.180
of the companies
I'm working with

01:29:35.180 --> 01:29:37.701
have their own queuing system.

01:29:37.701 --> 01:29:39.200
There's two different
ways to do it.

01:29:39.200 --> 01:29:41.020
One of them is to have
the queuing system

01:29:41.020 --> 01:29:43.510
dynamically manage the
spin up of new machines.

01:29:43.510 --> 01:29:45.010
Another way to do
is to actually use

01:29:45.010 --> 01:29:46.790
a script to spin up
machines and then

01:29:46.790 --> 01:29:48.920
add them to queuing system.

01:29:48.920 --> 01:29:52.220
It offers a little more manual
control doing it that way.

01:29:52.220 --> 01:29:54.460
And it just has to do
with whether you're

01:29:54.460 --> 01:29:57.187
using a commercial or off the
shelf-- oh, sorry, a commercial

01:29:57.187 --> 01:29:58.520
or roll your own queuing system.

01:29:58.520 --> 01:30:02.730
So those are kind of the
distinctions in that regard.

01:30:02.730 --> 01:30:05.720
It also comes back to the
production issue of who

01:30:05.720 --> 01:30:07.810
gets to control that valve.

01:30:07.810 --> 01:30:10.480
Most of the time it ends up
being someone in a CD sup

01:30:10.480 --> 01:30:13.250
position who talks with
production and controls

01:30:13.250 --> 01:30:15.327
how many machines
are brought online.

01:30:15.327 --> 01:30:16.785
A couple customers
I work with that

01:30:16.785 --> 01:30:18.690
were using a really
dynamic model where

01:30:18.690 --> 01:30:20.190
they're averaging their usage.

01:30:20.190 --> 01:30:23.250
So they've budgeted to
render like tens of thousands

01:30:23.250 --> 01:30:25.040
of cores on a sustained level.

01:30:25.040 --> 01:30:26.830
But they're able
to say, hey, I only

01:30:26.830 --> 01:30:30.430
want to run-- like if I'm good
for 10,000 cores for 24 hours,

01:30:30.430 --> 01:30:33.670
they'll run 20,000 cores for
12 and utilize the wall clock

01:30:33.670 --> 01:30:34.170
savings.

01:30:34.170 --> 01:30:36.420
So that's kind of more
advanced, but that's

01:30:36.420 --> 01:30:38.350
really beneficial for
the artist as well.

01:30:38.350 --> 01:30:39.470
TODD PRIVES: So I just
have one more question

01:30:39.470 --> 01:30:40.957
before we open up
to the audience.

01:30:40.957 --> 01:30:42.790
So having set up a cloud
rendering pipeline,

01:30:42.790 --> 01:30:44.730
what recommendations
can you give

01:30:44.730 --> 01:30:47.750
to those in the audience who
are looking to set something up

01:30:47.750 --> 01:30:49.500
based on your experience so far?

01:30:52.260 --> 01:30:55.630
DAMIEN BATAILLE: I
think for the pipeline,

01:30:55.630 --> 01:30:58.690
before we have to have good IT.

01:30:58.690 --> 01:31:09.330
And no, because for me, the
workflow render or the gerbil

01:31:09.330 --> 01:31:12.910
is exactly the same between
the cloud rendering.

01:31:12.910 --> 01:31:14.840
This is completely
transparent for me.

01:31:14.840 --> 01:31:19.090
So I didn't have to do a lot
of change inside the pipeline,

01:31:19.090 --> 01:31:23.490
because of cloud rendering,
we know what we are using,

01:31:23.490 --> 01:31:26.470
what file we are to render.

01:31:26.470 --> 01:31:29.950
This is more an IT work
than a pipeline work.

01:31:29.950 --> 01:31:32.940
So it is why this is really
busy and to integrate

01:31:32.940 --> 01:31:34.790
to sign the pipeline.

01:31:34.790 --> 01:31:36.110
TODD PRIVES: Great, thanks.

