01:33:09.120 --> 01:33:11.690
AUDIENCE: What ISBs are
you guys here with in LA,

01:33:11.690 --> 01:33:13.550
and what bandwidth
are you working with?

01:33:13.550 --> 01:33:15.920
And was that ever a
question or an issue?

01:33:15.920 --> 01:33:18.260
JEFF KEMBER: Yeah, no,
we have really fat pipes

01:33:18.260 --> 01:33:20.210
here in LA One
Wiltshire for sure,

01:33:20.210 --> 01:33:22.160
and I can check the
network diagram to see.

01:33:22.160 --> 01:33:24.182
We have multiple
peering locations, but--

01:33:24.182 --> 01:33:25.640
AUDIENCE: From a
studio standpoint.

01:33:25.640 --> 01:33:27.473
JEFF KEMBER: Well, from
a studio standpoint,

01:33:27.473 --> 01:33:29.660
in terms of how large a
pipe can you get into us?

01:33:29.660 --> 01:33:30.485
Or--

01:33:30.485 --> 01:33:31.860
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
is that a concern

01:33:31.860 --> 01:33:34.443
at all when you're dealing with
the cloud rendering and stuff?

01:33:34.443 --> 01:33:35.680
JEFF KEMBER: So- yeah, go.

01:33:35.680 --> 01:33:37.440
PHILIPPE CHOTARD:
So we at Eight VFX,

01:33:37.440 --> 01:33:43.540
we have a basic 100
megabit link that

01:33:43.540 --> 01:33:47.440
can have sometimes
very bad latency.

01:33:47.440 --> 01:33:53.410
So I've noticed that when
we have under 70 millisecond

01:33:53.410 --> 01:33:59.720
latency, you will have great
transfer even using SMB.

01:33:59.720 --> 01:34:04.950
And if you have over let's
say 100 millisecond latency,

01:34:04.950 --> 01:34:06.890
you have to use NFS.

01:34:06.890 --> 01:34:11.970
And we can-- when we
do some basic transfer

01:34:11.970 --> 01:34:17.460
between on-premise and the
cloud, we can max out the link.

01:34:17.460 --> 01:34:23.820
And so when we transfer
it, that with the cache

01:34:23.820 --> 01:34:27.390
Avere we just use all
the available bandwidth.

01:34:27.390 --> 01:34:31.310
We don't have--
now with Avere, we

01:34:31.310 --> 01:34:33.650
don't have any real
bandwidth issue.

01:34:33.650 --> 01:34:36.310
We did before because
all the renders would use

01:34:36.310 --> 01:34:39.910
that single 100 megabyte link.

01:34:39.910 --> 01:34:45.930
In the future, we would
like to use a better link.

01:34:45.930 --> 01:34:51.240
Not more speed, because
100 megabits is enough.

01:34:51.240 --> 01:34:54.690
But maybe with a lower latency.

01:34:54.690 --> 01:34:57.020
JEFF KEMBER: We just
have, for the data point,

01:34:57.020 --> 01:35:00.530
one customer in Central Asia was
doing a 60+ 1,000 core render.

01:35:00.530 --> 01:35:04.230
They had a 250 megabits
symmetric pipe with Avere.

01:35:04.230 --> 01:35:08.780
And they were rendering in Iowa,
so 140, 150 milliseconds away.

01:35:08.780 --> 01:35:11.880
We also have customers with
dual bonded 10 gig pipes

01:35:11.880 --> 01:35:13.280
into us as well.

01:35:13.280 --> 01:35:14.750
So it runs the whole gamut.

