We use like 14 [INAUDIBLE].

01:35:51.140 --> 01:35:55.400
And for little
project, this is OK.

01:35:55.400 --> 01:35:58.160
But when we know that
this is a big project,

01:35:58.160 --> 01:36:00.090
and when we are
doing a bid, we know

01:36:00.090 --> 01:36:04.200
that we have to get-- we have
to do a lot of rendering.

01:36:04.200 --> 01:36:06.990
It is here that we
decide OK, for this shirt

01:36:06.990 --> 01:36:10.220
or for this sequence, we
will use the Cloud rendering.

01:36:13.000 --> 01:36:17.260
PHILIPPE CHOTARD: Until
now, until a few months ago,

01:36:17.260 --> 01:36:22.630
we were using 100%
local rendering.

01:36:22.630 --> 01:36:25.450
But now we know
that for the future,

01:36:25.450 --> 01:36:30.580
we won't do as many render
nodes as we were planning to,

01:36:30.580 --> 01:36:36.210
because we have this online
resource available all

01:36:36.210 --> 01:36:37.020
the time.

01:36:37.020 --> 01:36:41.350
So we will be moving
office in a few months.

01:36:41.350 --> 01:36:47.040
And the rendering, the
sizing of the power

01:36:47.040 --> 01:36:51.710
of all the racks for the
render is not as a big issue

01:36:51.710 --> 01:36:55.800
as it was before
we use the Cloud.

01:36:55.800 --> 01:36:58.370
DAMIEN BATAILLE: Yeah, he
give us a good security,

01:36:58.370 --> 01:37:00.800
because before he was
a little bit scared

01:37:00.800 --> 01:37:05.724
to get some big project because
we did not have any Cloud

01:37:05.724 --> 01:37:07.390
renderings, so we
didn't know if you can

01:37:07.390 --> 01:37:09.050
render all the project on it.

01:37:09.050 --> 01:37:15.140
But now because we know that,
we have unlimited [INAUDIBLE]

01:37:15.140 --> 01:37:18.325
we didn't have an
affinity to render.

01:37:20.949 --> 01:37:21.740
TODD PRIVES: Great.

01:37:21.740 --> 01:37:22.900
Question here.

01:37:22.900 --> 01:37:25.560
AUDIENCE: Mike Shadow with
Deluxe Creative Services.

01:37:25.560 --> 01:37:28.880
You have multiple locations
at Eight VFX I saw.

01:37:28.880 --> 01:37:32.500
And do you share projects
across those locations

01:37:32.500 --> 01:37:33.770
that have used the Cloud?

01:37:33.770 --> 01:37:36.750
And are there issues that
you didn't anticipate?

01:37:36.750 --> 01:37:39.510
PHILIPPE CHOTARD: Currently
we-- Cloud rendering

01:37:39.510 --> 01:37:41.410
is working in New York.

01:37:41.410 --> 01:37:46.630
So now, we have a location
in Los Angeles, in New York,

01:37:46.630 --> 01:37:49.290
and in London.

01:37:49.290 --> 01:37:53.460
Currently, the only location
that is using cloud rendering

01:37:53.460 --> 01:37:56.010
is Los Angeles.

01:37:56.010 --> 01:37:59.270
We share project between
New York and Los Angeles.

01:37:59.270 --> 01:38:05.330
The pipes Damien wrote is able
to synchronize all the assets

01:38:05.330 --> 01:38:07.860
between the facilities.

01:38:07.860 --> 01:38:11.020
It's funny that you ask this
question because this morning,

01:38:11.020 --> 01:38:17.630
our CEO ask us, can we do
cloud rendering in New York?

01:38:17.630 --> 01:38:20.970
I said, yes because
it's exactly the same.

01:38:20.970 --> 01:38:26.230
Basically, in Los Angeles
on the artist point of view,

01:38:26.230 --> 01:38:28.850
you just click one
button to say, OK,

01:38:28.850 --> 01:38:30.670
I want to render on the cloud.

01:38:30.670 --> 01:38:34.400
Clicking this button will add
your job in a specific group.

01:38:34.400 --> 01:38:38.500
So after that deadline, we
say, OK, this is render cloud.

01:38:38.500 --> 01:38:40.980
I will first begin
to render locally.

01:38:40.980 --> 01:38:42.850
If I don't have
enough power locally,

01:38:42.850 --> 01:38:46.770
then I will fire certain
amounts up for cloud rendering.

01:38:46.770 --> 01:38:51.230
So this is exactly the same
since halfpipe is the same

01:38:51.230 --> 01:38:54.935
in Los Angeles and New York.

01:38:54.935 --> 01:38:55.810
They can do the same.

01:38:55.810 --> 01:38:57.760
They didn't do it yet,
but they can do it.

01:38:57.760 --> 01:38:59.530
The only thing they
have to do before,

01:38:59.530 --> 01:39:03.710
of course, obviously, is to set
up a VPN between the facility

01:39:03.710 --> 01:39:06.650
to our cloud network.

01:39:06.650 --> 01:39:09.205
And, yeah, it should be the
same, should be all the same.

01:39:09.205 --> 01:39:10.580
DAMIEN BATAILLE:
Currently we are

01:39:10.580 --> 01:39:14.650
using Aspera to synchronize all
the assets and all the pipeline

01:39:14.650 --> 01:39:16.942
between New York
and Los Angeles.

01:39:20.240 --> 01:39:21.950
TODD PRIVES: Any
other questions?

01:39:21.950 --> 01:39:23.860
Chris.

01:39:23.860 --> 01:39:25.820
AUDIENCE: I have a question.

01:39:25.820 --> 01:39:28.430
Obviously you've started to
replace part of your render

01:39:28.430 --> 01:39:30.805
farmer with Cloud, and it's
been a good solution for you,

01:39:30.805 --> 01:39:32.830
and obviously a replacement
for the rental model

01:39:32.830 --> 01:39:34.110
that you probably had before.

01:39:34.110 --> 01:39:37.110
But do you see yourself
moving completely to Cloud?

01:39:37.110 --> 01:39:40.310
And not only that but actually
replacing the workstations

01:39:40.310 --> 01:39:42.850
with Cloud machines
down the line

01:39:42.850 --> 01:39:46.520
where you can actually
just VPN or just

01:39:46.520 --> 01:39:49.420
get into the machine remotely?

01:39:49.420 --> 01:39:53.790
PHILIPPE CHOTARD: So for
the rendering point of view,

01:39:53.790 --> 01:39:57.850
we still want to have
some local render power.

01:39:57.850 --> 01:40:00.750
We don't want to put
everything on the cloud,

01:40:00.750 --> 01:40:06.580
because our VFX studio, we do
rendering, at least some hours

01:40:06.580 --> 01:40:08.050
of rendering every day.

01:40:08.050 --> 01:40:10.620
So on the cost
point of view, it's

01:40:10.620 --> 01:40:13.320
more interesting to have
at least a certain amount

01:40:13.320 --> 01:40:18.190
of local rendering power and to
have the ability on the Cloud

01:40:18.190 --> 01:40:21.200
to have more power,
availability.

01:40:21.200 --> 01:40:23.550
So this is for the
rendering aspect.

01:40:23.550 --> 01:40:26.310
After that-- so you asked
for the workstation, right?

01:40:26.310 --> 01:40:31.160
We are currently testing some
solution with [INAUDIBLE]

01:40:31.160 --> 01:40:34.530
maybe this technology
that's will--

01:40:34.530 --> 01:40:42.070
it's a [INAUDIBLE] solution
that is quite cheap compared

01:40:42.070 --> 01:40:45.370
to the [INAUDIBLE] solution.

01:40:45.370 --> 01:40:47.170
And that will allow
you to connect

01:40:47.170 --> 01:40:51.100
to not only physical
distant machine but also

01:40:51.100 --> 01:40:53.650
virtual machines.

01:40:53.650 --> 01:40:56.160
So until now, we've
done some testing

01:40:56.160 --> 01:41:02.660
with not artist workstation
because of the GPU stuff.

01:41:02.660 --> 01:41:06.960
But on the classical
station, producable station,

01:41:06.960 --> 01:41:11.620
connected either to
physical workstation

01:41:11.620 --> 01:41:15.910
or virtual workstation,
using VMware, it works.

01:41:15.910 --> 01:41:20.230
And now with the new
version of VMware

01:41:20.230 --> 01:41:23.960
that is ending that stuff,
they will enter the GPUs.

01:41:23.960 --> 01:41:28.590
So we are really thinking
about in the future, immediate

01:41:28.590 --> 01:41:33.580
future, to neutralize some
of the artist workstation

01:41:33.580 --> 01:41:37.890
using PC over IP solutions.

01:41:37.890 --> 01:41:43.000
So I didn't think
yet about if we

01:41:43.000 --> 01:41:48.010
will do it on whole local data
center or on the Google Cloud.

01:41:48.010 --> 01:41:51.290
But yeah, I will test
this very soon, I guess.

01:41:55.132 --> 01:41:57.340
AUDIENCE: Do you have a
figure in your head on what's

01:41:57.340 --> 01:42:00.850
the maximum latency that
will be acceptable for artist

01:42:00.850 --> 01:42:01.730
workstation?

01:42:01.730 --> 01:42:06.320
PHILIPPE CHOTARD: Yeah, so it's
about the same latency I talked

01:42:06.320 --> 01:42:12.440
about before, 70 millisecond
is when your artists will begin

01:42:12.440 --> 01:42:17.440
to bitch about the latency.

01:42:17.440 --> 01:42:20.660
They won't notice before.

01:42:20.660 --> 01:42:25.220
Actually last week,
I've put an artists

01:42:25.220 --> 01:42:29.220
on the PC over IP set up
without telling him anything.

01:42:29.220 --> 01:42:35.120
So there was a workstation right
near him that was shut off.

01:42:35.120 --> 01:42:36.620
It was not running.

01:42:36.620 --> 01:42:38.370
And yeah, it was working.

01:42:38.370 --> 01:42:41.110
And he didn't know he
was not working locally

01:42:41.110 --> 01:42:44.070
but on the virtual machine.

01:42:44.070 --> 01:42:46.012
JEFF KEMBER: We have
run some tests-- well,

01:42:46.012 --> 01:42:48.345
I shouldn't say we, but I
have customers who run in here

01:42:48.345 --> 01:42:49.053
about some tests.

01:42:49.053 --> 01:42:50.960
And they echo that as well.

01:42:50.960 --> 01:42:55.120
It's typically the Mari artists
who are most temperamental.

01:42:55.120 --> 01:42:58.530
Every brush stroke gets written,
so they want SSDs locally.

01:42:58.530 --> 01:43:03.550
But lighters and everyone else
can certainly be pushed off.

01:43:03.550 --> 01:43:06.400
The testing other customers
have done suggests a 20 to 40

01:43:06.400 --> 01:43:07.440
millisecond.

01:43:07.440 --> 01:43:10.180
But we've also had
numbers as high as 80

01:43:10.180 --> 01:43:13.180
with artists who
were doing modeling

01:43:13.180 --> 01:43:15.026
when you're pulling verts.

01:43:15.026 --> 01:43:17.150
That and painting, those
are the two critical ones.

01:43:17.150 --> 01:43:19.380
But we've had compers
running at 100 milliseconds

01:43:19.380 --> 01:43:20.460
without a problem.

01:43:20.460 --> 01:43:23.220
So I think there's some variance
within the job functions.

